const t=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"revelation-20","title":"The Thousand Years","ref":"Revelation 20:1–10","ranges":["Revelation 20:1-10"],"category":"revelation","summary":"An angel binds Satan for a thousand years so that he can no longer deceive the nations; enthroned souls “came to life” (ezēsan) and reign with Christ in the “first resurrection”; afterwards Satan is released, gathers Gog and Magog, and is cast into the lake of fire. The only passage in Scripture that names a thousand-year period, it gives the millennial views their names.","keyIssue":"Does Revelation 20 follow the return of Christ in Revelation 19 chronologically (premillennial), or does it recapitulate and describe the whole period between the two advents (amillennial and most postmillennial readings)? Closely tied are what “bound” means (compare Matthew 12:29, where Jesus binds the strong man), whether ezēsan in 20:4 and 20:5 must mean bodily resurrection both times, and whether “thousand” is literal or symbolic.","timeText":false,"topics":["millennium","satan-binding","first-resurrection","second-coming","final-judgment"],"readings":[{"position":"postmil","reading":"The thousand years is the long, symbolically numbered gospel age, begun at Christ's first advent and enthronement. Satan is bound so he cannot stop the gospel from discipling the nations (20:3), and the age moves, through the Spirit's work, toward a historical flourishing of the faith before a brief final rebellion (20:7-9) and Christ's return. Warfield read the reigning souls as the saints in heaven (the intermediate state); Gentry and Boettner stress the gradual triumph of the kingdom on earth that the binding makes possible.","support":"The binding language echoes Jesus' own claim to have bound the strong man (Matthew 12:29) and to see Satan fall (Luke 10:18; John 12:31-32), and the stated purpose of the binding is specifically about the nations, which matches the Great Commission. “Thousand” is used elsewhere for a vast, complete number (Psalm 50:10; Deuteronomy 7:9). Postmillennialists add that other texts (Psalm 110, 1 Corinthians 15:25, Matthew 13:31-33) predict growing dominion, so the millennium is naturally that era of growth.","objection":"Premillennialists reply that Satan is still described as active, prowling and deceiving in the New Testament (1 Peter 5:8; 2 Corinthians 4:4), and the abyss is sealed over him, which they think is far stronger than restriction. Amillennialists, who share the recapitulation reading, object that Revelation 20 itself says nothing about a future golden age within history; the visible reign is in heaven, and the age ends in global apostasy (20:8).","sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","boettner-millennium","warfield-millennium-apocalypse"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"Revelation 20:1 begins a new vision that goes back to the first advent, as Revelation 12 does. The thousand years is the whole church age. Satan's binding is his restraint from stopping the gospel among the nations or gathering them for the final battle before the appointed time. The “first resurrection” is either regeneration (Augustine) or, more often today, the believer's entrance into heavenly life with Christ at death (Hoekema, Kline, Riddlebarger, Storms); the second, bodily resurrection comes at Christ's single return, followed by the final judgment.","support":"The final battle of 20:8 is described with the same Ezekiel 38-39 imagery and the same article (“the war”) as 16:14 and 19:19, which suggests it is the same event seen again. The thrones of 20:4 are in heaven, as elsewhere in Revelation, and the persons are “souls”. John 5:24-29 has the same pattern of a spiritual resurrection now and a bodily one at the end. The rest of the New Testament teaches one return, one general resurrection and one judgment (John 5:28-29; Acts 24:15; Matthew 25:31-46).","objection":"Since Henry Alford, premillennialists have pressed that ezēsan cannot mean spiritual life in 20:4 and bodily life in 20:5 within the same passage, and that the word anastasis almost always means bodily resurrection. They also argue that 19:20 and 20:10 link the two chapters in sequence: the beast and false prophet are already in the lake of fire when the devil joins them.","sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil","storms-millennium-revelation-20","augustine-city-of-god"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"The visions run in order: Christ returns (19:11-21), Satan is then bound, and the martyrs and faithful are bodily raised to reign with Christ on earth for a long period (whether exactly a thousand years or not). After the rebellion at the end of that period come the resurrection of the rest of the dead and the final judgment. This is the hope of early church writers such as Papias, Justin and Irenaeus, held without the dispensational distinction between Israel and the church.","support":"The plain sequence of the narrative and the parallel use of ezēsan: if the second “came to life” (20:5) is bodily, so is the first. 1 Corinthians 15:23-24 seems to allow an interval between the resurrection of “those who belong to Christ” and “the end”. The interpretation is also old: Justin (Dialogue 80-81) and Irenaeus (Against Heresies V) held an earthly kingdom after Christ's return.","objection":"Amillennialists and postmillennialists answer that Revelation is a series of parallel visions rather than a single timeline, and 20:1 starts a fresh vision. They also ask why, if Christ has returned and the nations have been destroyed (19:18-21), there are still nations to deceive in 20:3 and 20:8, and why no other New Testament text clearly teaches two bodily resurrections separated by an age.","sources":["ladd-commentary-revelation","blomberg-chung-historic-premil","irenaeus-against-heresies","justin-dialogue-trypho"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"The thousand years is a literal future era after the Second Coming in which Christ reigns from Jerusalem on David's throne. In that era God fulfils his unconditional covenant promises to national Israel (land, kingdom, temple), and the raptured church reigns with him. The binding is a complete removal of Satan's activity from the earth. The first resurrection happens in stages (Christ, the raptured church, tribulation martyrs, Old Testament saints), and the rest of the dead are raised to the great white throne judgment afterwards.","support":"Six repetitions of “a thousand years” in a few verses suggest a real duration. The binding is described in heavy terms (seized, bound, thrown into the abyss, shut and sealed), which does not fit Satan's current activity. The reading also fits a consistent literal hermeneutic that expects the Old Testament kingdom prophecies (Isaiah 2; 11; 65; Zechariah 14) to be fulfilled on earth, in history, for Israel.","objection":"Critics observe that numbers in Revelation are overwhelmingly symbolic (seven spirits, 144,000, 666), and that Revelation 20 itself says nothing about Israel, Jerusalem's temple, or the land. They argue that importing those elements from the prophets presupposes the dispensational system rather than deriving it from this text.","sources":["walvoord-millennial-kingdom","pentecost-things-to-come","bock-three-views"]},{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"Partial preterists (usually postmillennial, as Gentry, or amillennial, as some others) read Revelation 4-19 as chiefly fulfilled in the first-century judgment on Jerusalem and the persecuting Roman beast, and see Revelation 20 as looking beyond that to the long era of Christ's reign that began with his first coming. The martyrs under Nero are vindicated and reign; the binding began with Christ's ministry and cross; the final release of Satan and the end are still future.","support":"Revelation's repeated time indicators (“soon”, “the time is near”, 1:1, 1:3, 22:6, 22:10) govern the bulk of the prophecy, while Revelation 20 explicitly introduces a long period (a thousand years), which marks it off from the near events. The martyr language in 20:4 (beheaded for the testimony of Jesus, not worshipping the beast) ties it to the persecutions earlier in the book.","objection":"Futurists challenge the early (pre-70) dating of Revelation that the preterist frame usually relies on; most scholars favour c. AD 95 on the basis of Irenaeus (the date is disputed). Full preterists, from the other side, argue it is inconsistent to apply “soon” to Revelation 1-19 and then put the resurrection and judgment of 20:11-15 thousands of years later.","sources":["gentry-before-jerusalem-fell","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","gregg-revelation-four-views"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"All of Revelation, including chapter 20, was fulfilled by the AD 70 destruction of Jerusalem. Many full preterists take the thousand years as the transitional generation between the cross and AD 70 (or a symbolic era closing then). The first resurrection is the new life of believers in Christ, and the judgment and emptying of Hades (20:13-14) took place at the parousia in AD 70. There is no future bodily resurrection or end of history.","support":"Proponents appeal to the time statements in the frame of the book (1:1-3; 22:6-12, “do not seal up the prophecy... the time is near”), and to Daniel 12:2 and 12:7, which they say tie the resurrection to the shattering of the holy people's power. They argue that their view applies “near” consistently to the whole book.","objection":"Critics, including partial preterists, note that this view denies a future bodily resurrection and visible Second Coming, which all the ecumenical creeds confess (the Nicene Creed: he “will come again... to judge the living and the dead”), and so stands outside historic orthodoxy. They also argue it has to compress a “thousand years” into roughly forty. Proponents answer that Scripture outranks creeds and that the creeds did not anticipate the time-statement argument.","sources":["king-spirit-of-prophecy","mathison-when-shall","nicene-creed"]},{"position":"historicism","reading":"Historicists read Revelation as a continuous prophecy of church history from John's day to the end, with Revelation 20 coming after the fall of the papal “beast” and Antichrist. Many historicists (Elliott) were premillennial and placed the thousand years after a future return; others (Barnes, and much of the Puritan and post-Reformation tradition) expected a future era of gospel triumph after the overthrow of papal Rome, before Christ returns.","support":"The book's numbered sequences (seals, trumpets, bowls) look like a progressive history, and the year-day principle (Numbers 14:34; Ezekiel 4:6) allows long historical periods. To historicists, the record of church history read alongside the chapters confirms the scheme.","objection":"Critics point out that historicist identifications have changed with each generation and have often set dates that failed. The scheme also tends to centre Western European history in a way that John's first readers could not have understood.","sources":["elliott-horae","barnes-notes-revelation","murray-puritan-hope"]},{"position":"idealism","reading":"Revelation presents recurring spiritual realities rather than datable events. Following Hendriksen's “progressive parallelism”, chapter 20 is the last of seven parallel sections, each covering the age from the first to the second advent. The binding says Satan cannot halt the gathering of the nations through the gospel; the reigning souls depict the present victory of the faithful dead; Gog and Magog depict the final, intensified opposition that the church must always expect.","support":"Revelation's genre is apocalyptic, full of symbols, and it is addressed to seven real churches that needed comfort for their own day and every day. The cycles of judgment in the book visibly repeat and end in the final judgment more than once (6:12-17; 11:15-18; 16:17-21; 19:11-21; 20:11-15).","objection":"Critics say that idealism risks detaching the visions from any real history, although Revelation claims to show “what must soon take place” and predicts a concrete return, resurrection and judgment. Most idealists answer that they affirm those final events and read only the intervening symbols as transhistorical.","sources":["hendriksen-more-than-conquerors","beale-revelation-nigtc","pate-four-views-revelation"]}],"sources":["clouse-meaning-millennium","bock-three-views","gregg-revelation-four-views","beale-revelation-nigtc"]},{"id":"matthew-24","title":"The Olivet Discourse (Matthew)","ref":"Matthew 24:1–51","ranges":["Matthew 24:1-51"],"category":"olivet","summary":"After Jesus predicts the temple's destruction, the disciples ask when it will happen and what will be the sign of his coming (parousia) and of the end of the age (synteleia tou aiōnos). Jesus describes false messiahs, wars, persecution, the gospel going to the whole world, the “abomination of desolation”, great tribulation, cosmic signs and the Son of Man coming on the clouds. He then says “this generation” (hē genea hautē) will not pass away until all these things take place (24:34), but that no one knows the day or hour (24:36).","keyIssue":"What “this generation” (genea) in 24:34 means, and whether the discourse moves at 24:36 from the AD 70 destruction to the final coming. Nearly every reading depends on how the timing word in 24:34 relates to the coming of the Son of Man in 24:30 (quoting Daniel 7:13) and to the parousia language in 24:27, 37 and 39.","timeText":true,"topics":["olivet-generation","great-tribulation","second-coming","rapture","antichrist"],"readings":[{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"Matthew 24:4-34 predicts the events of AD 66-70. “This generation” means Jesus' contemporaries, as it does every other time in Matthew (11:16; 12:41-45; 23:36). The abomination of desolation is the Roman (and Zealot) desecration of the temple; the great tribulation is the siege of Jerusalem; and the Son of Man “coming on the clouds” (24:30) is Daniel 7:13 imagery for Christ's vindication and enthronement, shown in judgment on the city. Most partial preterists (Gentry, Sproul, DeMar, France) see a shift at 24:36 to the still-future final coming.","support":"The disciples' question is set off by the temple prediction (24:1-3). Within Matthew the timing is tied together: 23:36 says judgment will fall on “this generation”, 16:28 says some standing there will not taste death, and 26:64 says the high priest will “see” the Son of Man coming. In the prophets, cosmic collapse is standard imagery for the fall of nations (Isaiah 13:10; 34:4), and the details of the siege recorded by Josephus closely fit.","objection":"Futurists reply that Jesus describes the coming as universal and visible, like lightning (24:27), followed by a worldwide gathering of the elect (24:31), and that the tribulation is called unequalled in all history (24:21). The contrast between “these things” and “that day” is also disputed, since parousia appears on both sides of 24:36 (24:27, 37, 39).","sources":["sproul-last-days","demar-last-days-madness","france-matthew-nicnt","gentry-ice-great-tribulation"]},{"position":"postmil","reading":"Most modern postmillennialists hold the partial-preterist reading of 24:1-34 and stress what it means for history. The great tribulation is behind the church, not ahead of it. The gospel was preached to the whole oikoumenē before AD 70 (24:14; compare Colossians 1:6, 23), and the end of the old covenant order leaves the kingdom free to grow. Kik divided the discourse at 24:35/36: the first part is AD 70, the second the unannounced Second Coming at the end of history.","support":"Paul uses the same language of universal proclamation about his own day (Romans 1:8; 10:18; Colossians 1:23). Removing a future global tribulation takes away the main textual objection to an optimistic view of the church age, and 24:36-51 still teaches watchfulness for a future coming.","objection":"Amillennialists and premillennialists argue that the signs (deception, lawlessness, persecution) are presented as typical of the whole age until the end, so the discourse does not support optimism even if AD 70 is in view. Futurists add that 24:14 ties the gospel's spread to “the end” (to telos), not to the temple's fall.","sources":["kik-eschatology-of-victory","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","mathison-postmillennialism"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"The discourse answers both questions together. AD 70 is a real, near fulfilment and also a type of the final judgment, so the signs are both fulfilled in the first century and repeated through the whole age. The tribulation, deception and false Christs mark the entire interadvental period and intensify before the end. Amillennialists differ on details: Hoekema treats the signs as present throughout history; Riddlebarger accepts a preterist reading of much of 24:4-34 while holding 24:29-31 and 36-51 as future.","support":"Prophetic “foreshortening” is common in the Old Testament: Isaiah speaks of the return from Babylon and the messianic age in one breath. The disciples assumed the temple's fall meant the end of the age, and Jesus answers both without separating them sharply, while still saying the end is not yet (24:6).","objection":"Preterists object that “all these things” in 24:34 forbids double reference: the whole set of signs must occur within the generation. Dispensationalists object that a vague, age-long tribulation empties the specific language of 24:15-21 (a single event, a specific place, a flight from Judea).","sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"Jesus looks at two events through one lens: the near destruction of Jerusalem and the end of the age, with the second still future. The tribulation of 24:21-29 culminates at the end in a final crisis, which the church will go through. The coming of 24:30-31 is the single posttribulational return, when the angels gather the elect with a trumpet (the same gathering as 1 Thessalonians 4:16-17). On 24:34, writers such as Blomberg take “all these things” to mean the preliminary signs of 24:4-28, all of which had begun before AD 70, while the coming itself is open-ended.","support":"The return comes “immediately after” the tribulation (24:29), and the elect are gathered then (24:31), not before, which fits a posttribulational return. Luke's parallel clarifies that a long Gentile period follows the fall of Jerusalem (Luke 21:24). The “trumpet” and “gathering” match Paul's rapture language.","objection":"Preterists ask how “all these things” in 24:33-34 can exclude the coming in 24:30, which is the climax of the list. Dispensationalists argue that the setting is Jewish (Judea, the Sabbath, 24:16, 20) and concerns Israel, not the church.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","blomberg-chung-historic-premil","hultberg-three-views-rapture"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"Most of the discourse describes the future seven-year tribulation, especially its second half (24:15-28), which begins when the Antichrist sets up the abomination in a rebuilt temple (Daniel 9:27; 2 Thessalonians 2:4). The passage addresses believing Israel in that period, not the church, which will already have been raptured. “This generation” means the generation alive when these signs begin (Walvoord, Ice), or alternatively “this race” of Israel (Scofield). The Son of Man's coming in 24:30 is the visible Second Coming to set up the millennial kingdom.","support":"Jesus himself links the abomination to Daniel (24:15), which dispensationalists place in the future seventieth week. The scale of the tribulation (“such as has not been from the beginning of the world”, 24:21) and the cosmic signs exceed AD 70. The surrounding parables (24:32-33, the fig tree) describe the generation that “sees all these things”.","objection":"Critics argue that genea elsewhere in Matthew always means Jesus' contemporaries, and that the reading “the generation that sees these signs” is not natural Greek usage. Luke's parallel (Luke 21:20) interprets the abomination as Jerusalem surrounded by armies, which points to AD 70. Pre-tribulational placement of the church outside the discourse is also contested by historic premillennialists.","sources":["walvoord-millennial-kingdom","pentecost-things-to-come","gentry-ice-great-tribulation"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"The whole discourse, including 24:36-51, was fulfilled within the generation that heard it. There is one question, one coming and one end of the age (the end of the old covenant age), and “this generation” governs all of it. The parousia, the gathering of the elect and the end of the age took place in AD 70, so no future visible return remains.","support":"Full preterists stress that the disciples' question is a single question with linked parts (24:3), that “all these things” (24:34) includes the coming of 24:30, and that parousia is used both before and after 24:36. They argue that dividing the discourse at 24:36 is an inconsistency on the part of partial preterists.","objection":"Partial preterists and futurists answer that 24:36 begins a contrast (peri de, “but concerning”) between datable signs and an unannounced day. They also point to other texts that present the return as bodily and visible (Acts 1:11) and that tie it to the general resurrection (1 Corinthians 15:23). Critics also note that this view sits outside the creedal confession of a future return to judge the living and the dead.","sources":["king-spirit-of-prophecy","mathison-when-shall"]}],"sources":["gentry-ice-great-tribulation","france-matthew-nicnt","sproul-last-days","walvoord-millennial-kingdom"]},{"id":"mark-13","title":"The Olivet Discourse (Mark)","ref":"Mark 13:1–37","ranges":["Mark 13:1-37"],"category":"olivet","summary":"Mark's shorter version, probably the earliest. The disciples ask only when the temple will be destroyed and what the sign will be that “all these things” are about to be accomplished (13:4). Jesus warns of deceivers and persecution, says the gospel must first be preached to all nations (13:10), speaks of the abomination “standing where he ought not” (13:14), describes cosmic signs and the Son of Man coming with great power, says this generation will not pass until all these things happen (13:30), and ends with a call to watch because no one knows the day or hour (13:32-37).","keyIssue":"Mark's version of the question is only about the temple and “these things”, and there is no parousia language. Preterists take this as evidence that the discourse is about AD 70; futurists see the same structure as Matthew (signs, then a final coming) and take 13:32 as the turning point toward the last day.","timeText":true,"topics":["olivet-generation","great-tribulation","second-coming","antichrist"],"readings":[{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"Mark's framing (13:1-4) is entirely about the temple, so 13:5-31 describes AD 70. The masculine participle in 13:14 (hestēkota, “he” standing) suggests a person or army desecrating the sanctuary. 13:24-27 uses Isaiah's and Daniel's imagery for the fall of the old order and the Son of Man's vindication, with the “gathering of the elect” referring to the worldwide mission. Many partial preterists see 13:32-37 as moving to the final day.","support":"Without the parousia question that appears in Matthew, the natural antecedent of “these things” in 13:4, 13:29 and 13:30 is the temple's destruction. Jesus' warning to flee to the mountains (13:14) fits Judea under siege and makes little sense for a global end-time event.","objection":"Futurists reply that 13:24-27 introduces a decisive, visible coming “with great power and glory” and a gathering “from the ends of the earth”, which goes well beyond AD 70, and that Mark's readers would have heard Daniel 7:13 as a coming at the end. Full preterists object that the break at 13:32 is arbitrary.","sources":["wright-jesus-victory","sproul-last-days","demar-last-days-madness"]},{"position":"postmil","reading":"Postmillennialists generally share the partial-preterist reading. They emphasise 13:10, which says the gospel must first be preached to all nations before the fall of Jerusalem, and stress that the discourse warns about the first-century crisis rather than predicting the course of the whole church age. Christ's present reign continues after AD 70, and the church's task of discipling the nations goes on.","support":"The emphasis is on local events (Judea, housetops, winter) and the disciples' own persecution before councils and synagogues (13:9), which fits the first century. Acts and Paul's letters describe the gospel reaching the nations within that generation.","objection":"Critics say the passage gives no positive picture of the later age at all; postmillennial optimism has to come from other texts. Futurists also question whether 13:10 was fully realised before AD 70.","sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","kik-eschatology-of-victory"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"The discourse mixes near and far horizons. The temple's destruction is a type, a preview, of the final judgment, and the dangers it describes (deception, persecution, false messiahs) continue throughout the age. 13:24-27 points to the Second Coming, and the call to watch (13:33-37) applies to every generation of the church.","support":"The parable of the doorkeeper (13:34-37) addresses “all” (13:37) and assumes a long absence of the master, which suggests that the discourse looks beyond the first generation.","objection":"Preterists argue that the same “all” in 13:30 limits the whole set of signs to one generation, so the two horizons cannot be separated as easily as the amillennial reading suggests.","sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"Historic premillennialists read Mark 13 much as Ladd did: Jesus spoke of the fall of Jerusalem and of the end of the age without clearly separating them in time. The near event fulfilled the first horizon, and the final tribulation and posttribulational return still lie ahead. The gathering of the elect (13:27) is the church's resurrection and rapture at the end of the tribulation.","support":"The sequence “in those days, after that tribulation” (13:24) puts the coming after the tribulation, which fits a posttribulational rapture. The disciples are addressed as representatives of the church in the gospel's mission to all nations (13:10).","objection":"Pretribulational dispensationalists argue that the elect here are tribulation saints (often Jewish), not the raptured church. Preterists argue that “prophetic telescoping” does not answer the explicit time limit in 13:30.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","blomberg-chung-historic-premil"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"Mark 13:14-27 describes the future tribulation and the abomination set up by the Antichrist in a rebuilt temple, followed by Christ's visible coming. The “elect” are believers on earth during that period, especially the Jewish remnant. “This generation” means the one that sees these final signs.","support":"Jesus calls the tribulation unique in all history (13:19), and the coming involves cosmic upheaval and a universal gathering (13:27). Dispensationalists argue that these exceed anything that occurred in AD 70.","objection":"Critics answer that hyperbolic “never before” language is used of other local judgments in the Old Testament (Ezekiel 5:9; Daniel 9:12), and that Mark gives no sign of a two-thousand-year gap or a rebuilt temple.","sources":["walvoord-millennial-kingdom","pentecost-things-to-come"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"Every element of Mark 13, including the coming of the Son of Man and the gathering of the elect, was fulfilled by AD 70. Since the question in 13:4 asks only about “these things”, the whole answer refers to them, and 13:30 sets its limit.","support":"Full preterists treat Mark's wording as their strongest Olivet evidence, because there is no separate question about the end of the world, and they argue that the “day or hour” of 13:32 is the hour of the same event.","objection":"Critics answer that other New Testament texts link the coming to a bodily resurrection and a renewed creation (Romans 8:18-23; 1 Corinthians 15), neither of which occurred in AD 70. They also note that this position lies outside the historic creeds.","sources":["king-spirit-of-prophecy","mathison-when-shall"]}],"sources":["wright-jesus-victory","gentry-ice-great-tribulation","hoekema-bible-future"]},{"id":"luke-21","title":"The Olivet Discourse (Luke)","ref":"Luke 21:5–36","ranges":["Luke 21:5-36"],"category":"olivet","summary":"Luke's account replaces the “abomination of desolation” with the plain warning “when you see Jerusalem surrounded by armies” (21:20). Jerusalem will be trampled by Gentiles “until the times of the Gentiles are fulfilled” (21:24). Then come signs in sun, moon and stars and the Son of Man coming in a cloud. Believers are to lift up their heads, because their redemption is drawing near (21:28); this generation will not pass away until all has taken place (21:32).","keyIssue":"Luke explicitly ties the abomination to the Roman siege of AD 70, which preterists take as the key to the parallels. But he also adds “the times of the Gentiles” (21:24), which seems to place an open-ended period between the city's fall and the coming of the Son of Man. Everything turns on how 21:24 relates to 21:25-28.","timeText":true,"topics":["olivet-generation","great-tribulation","israel","second-coming"],"readings":[{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"Luke is the inspired interpreter of the parallels: the desolation is the Roman siege, and the “days of vengeance” (21:22) are the fulfilment of the curses on covenant-breaking Jerusalem. Some partial preterists also read the cosmic signs and the coming in 21:25-28 as the AD 70 judgment-vindication, with “redemption” referring to the church's deliverance from its persecutors; others take 21:24 as a hinge and 21:25-36 as future.","support":"Luke's language is concrete and local (armies, Judea, those in the country, 21:20-21). The phrase “all that is written” being fulfilled (21:22) presents AD 70 as the climax of covenant judgment. The time text in 21:32 matches Matthew and Mark.","objection":"Futurists argue that the “times of the Gentiles” in 21:24 opens an indefinite period after AD 70, and that the coming of 21:27 comes after it, so 21:25-28 must be later than AD 70. Partial preterists are themselves divided on this point.","sources":["sproul-last-days","demar-last-days-madness","gentry-ice-great-tribulation"]},{"position":"postmil","reading":"With most partial preterists, postmillennialists see 21:20-24 as fulfilled in AD 70 and the “times of the Gentiles” as the long era of the gospel among the nations. Many (following the Puritans and Romans 11:25) expect that this era will eventually be followed by a large turning of the Jews to Christ, which will bring further blessing to the world.","support":"“Until” (achri) in 21:24 implies that Jerusalem's trampling has a limit, which parallels Paul's “until the fullness of the Gentiles has come in” (Romans 11:25). That supports the hope of a future ingathering of Israel within history.","objection":"Critics argue that the Romans 11 link is not clearly made by Luke and that 21:24 may simply refer to Gentile domination until the end, not to a period of growing gospel success.","sources":["murray-puritan-hope","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"Luke separates the two horizons more clearly than Matthew or Mark. 21:8-24 concerns AD 70; 21:24 opens the church age (the times of the Gentiles); 21:25-28 describes the Second Coming at its end. The discourse thus supports a single future return after an indefinite period, with no separate millennial era.","support":"The structure of the chapter is a near event (the siege), then an open-ended period, then the final coming, which matches the amillennial picture of the present age. The universal language of 21:35 (“all who dwell on the face of the whole earth”) points beyond Judea.","objection":"Preterists ask how 21:32 (“all has taken place”) can include an event thousands of years later; amillennial answers usually take “all” to refer to the signs leading up to the end, which preterists find strained.","sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"Luke shows the pattern that historic premillennialists find throughout the discourse: Jerusalem's fall, a long Gentile age, then the posttribulational return of Christ, at which believers' redemption, meaning bodily resurrection and the gathering of the church, arrives (21:28). Many also read 21:24 as implying a future restoration of Israel.","support":"The redemption of 21:28 parallels Romans 8:23 (the redemption of our bodies), suggesting the resurrection at the end. The open-ended times of the Gentiles makes room for a future coming after a long period.","objection":"Preterists press 21:32 again; dispensationalists argue that Luke's focus on Jerusalem points to a specifically Jewish future programme, which historic premillennialism does not develop.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","blomberg-chung-historic-premil"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"Dispensationalists usually concede that Luke 21:20-24 describes AD 70, unlike Matthew 24:15. They argue that Luke records a different part of Jesus' teaching, while 21:25-28 jumps forward to the end of the future tribulation and Christ's return. The “times of the Gentiles”, which began with Nebuchadnezzar and continue today, end when Christ restores Jerusalem and Israel.","support":"The “until” of 21:24 implies that Jerusalem will one day no longer be trampled, which fits national Israel's restoration. The different wording in Luke (armies rather than an abomination in the holy place) allows Matthew's abomination to remain a separate, future event.","objection":"Critics say that treating Luke 21:20 as a different event from the parallel Matthew 24:15 and Mark 13:14, which sit in the same place in the discourse with the same command to flee, is special pleading driven by the system.","sources":["walvoord-millennial-kingdom","pentecost-things-to-come","gentry-ice-great-tribulation"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"The whole chapter, including the coming in a cloud and the believers' redemption, was fulfilled within the generation (21:32). The times of the Gentiles are the short period of Roman trampling in AD 66-70, which ended with the consummation of the kingdom (21:31).","support":"Full preterists point to the time limit in 21:32 and the near-redemption language of 21:28, which is addressed to the disciples (“your redemption”).","objection":"Critics argue that the Gentile trampling of Jerusalem continued for centuries after AD 70, and that “redemption” elsewhere includes the bodily resurrection (Romans 8:23), which full preterism does not expect in a physical sense. They note that this view lies outside creedal orthodoxy.","sources":["king-spirit-of-prophecy","mathison-when-shall"]}],"sources":["gentry-ice-great-tribulation","sproul-last-days","ladd-blessed-hope"]},{"id":"daniel-2","title":"The Statue and the Stone","ref":"Daniel 2:31–45","ranges":["Daniel 2:31-45"],"category":"daniel","summary":"Nebuchadnezzar's dream shows a statue of gold, silver, bronze, iron and iron mixed with clay, representing successive kingdoms. A stone cut out “without hands” strikes the statue's feet, destroys it entirely and grows into a great mountain that fills the earth. Daniel explains that “in the days of those kings” God will set up a kingdom that will never be destroyed (2:44).","keyIssue":"When the stone strikes, and how the kingdom grows. If the stone is Christ's first advent in the days of Rome and the kingdom then grows gradually to fill the earth, the passage supports a present, expanding kingdom. If the stone strikes a future, revived form of the fourth kingdom suddenly at the Second Coming, it supports premillennialism. Conservative interpreters usually identify the kingdoms as Babylon, Medo-Persia, Greece and Rome; critical scholars usually make them Babylon, Media, Persia and Greece.","timeText":false,"topics":["kingdom","optimism","second-coming","church-history"],"readings":[{"position":"postmil","reading":"The stone is Christ and his kingdom, set up during the Roman empire at his first coming. Growing from a small stone into a mountain that fills the earth is a picture of the gradual, worldwide spread of his kingdom in history through the gospel, until it overwhelms all rival powers. Daniel 2 is thus one of postmillennialism's key Old Testament proofs of the kingdom's growth before the end.","support":"The kingdom is established “in the days of those kings” (2:44), meaning during the fourth empire, which fits Christ's announcement that the kingdom had come near (Mark 1:15). The growth image matches the parables of the mustard seed and leaven (Matthew 13:31-33).","objection":"Premillennialists answer that in the dream the stone first destroys the statue completely, so the destruction is sudden and comes before the growth, which does not fit gradual conversion. Amillennialists agree that the kingdom came at the first advent but doubt that the mountain describes a stage of history before the return.","sources":["boettner-millennium","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","kik-eschatology-of-victory"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"The stone is Christ's kingdom, inaugurated at his first advent during the Roman era. It is spiritual and heavenly in origin (“cut without hands”), already present, and will be consummated when Christ returns to destroy all opposing kingdoms. The mountain that fills the earth is the final, universal kingdom, partly realised now in the church among all nations and fully realised in the new creation.","support":"Jesus may allude to the stone in Luke 20:18, linking it to his own person. The kingdom “shall stand forever” (2:44), which suits a kingdom that runs straight from the first advent into eternity rather than a temporary millennium.","objection":"Postmillennialists say amillennialism does not take seriously the growth of the stone into a mountain within history. Premillennialists say it reads the crushing of the kingdoms as gradual or spiritual when the text describes a decisive blow.","sources":["young-prophecy-of-daniel","hoekema-bible-future"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"The kingdom of God was inaugurated by Christ, but the decisive smashing of world empires described by the stone comes at his return, which brings in the earthly kingdom. Ladd stresses the “already and not yet”: the kingdom's power is present now, but its visible triumph over the kingdoms of the world is still ahead.","support":"The stone does not coexist with the statue; it destroys it at one blow (2:34-35), which fits a cataclysmic return (compare Revelation 11:15; 19:15).","objection":"Critics note that the stone appears “in the days of those kings” (the fourth kingdom), which seems to fix its arrival in Roman times rather than in the distant future.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","grudem-systematic"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"The feet and ten toes represent a final, future phase of the Roman empire, a ten-king confederacy that has not yet existed. Christ, the stone, will strike it suddenly at his Second Coming, destroy Gentile world power and set up the millennial kingdom on earth, with Jerusalem as its centre. The kingdom does not grow gradually within the present age.","support":"The toes and the ten horns of Daniel 7:24 and Revelation 17:12 correspond, and no historical confederacy of ten kings fits them. The striking is catastrophic, not gradual, which matches the description of the return in Revelation 19.","objection":"Critics say Daniel gives no hint of a gap between the legs and the feet of the fourth kingdom, and the text dates the stone to the days of those kings. A revived Rome is read into the passage rather than drawn from it.","sources":["walvoord-daniel","pentecost-things-to-come"]},{"position":"historicism","reading":"The four kingdoms are Babylon, Medo-Persia, Greece and Rome; the mixed iron and clay are the divided kingdoms of Western Europe after Rome's fall, which have never been successfully joined by marriage alliances or conquest. Christ's kingdom, begun at his first coming, will finally replace them.","support":"Historicists (Newton, Barnes) point to the long-term failure of every attempt to reunite Europe (Charlemagne, Napoleon) as striking evidence that the prophecy is being fulfilled in history.","objection":"Critics argue that the European focus is a narrow reading of a prophecy about God's universal kingdom, and that historical identifications are elastic enough to fit almost any outcome.","sources":["newton-observations-daniel","barnes-notes-revelation"]},{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"The stone struck the fourth (Roman) kingdom in the first century: Christ came during the Roman empire, and his kingdom was established at his ascension and vindicated in the judgment of AD 70. Since then it grows into the mountain that fills the earth, as the Christian faith outlasts and replaces the old Roman order.","support":"The fourth empire is Rome, so the stone's coming has to be placed in the Roman era. Rome's own conversion and eventual collapse while the church continued are taken to illustrate the vision.","objection":"Futurists reply that Rome continued for centuries after the first advent and that the statue was not destroyed “all together” (2:35) in the first century.","sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","gregg-revelation-four-views"]}],"sources":["young-prophecy-of-daniel","walvoord-daniel","newton-observations-daniel"]},{"id":"daniel-7","title":"The Four Beasts and the Son of Man","ref":"Daniel 7","ranges":["Daniel 7:1-28"],"category":"daniel","summary":"Daniel sees four beasts rise from the sea. The fourth has ten horns, and a little horn among them speaks boastfully and wages war on the saints for “a time, times and half a time” (7:25). The Ancient of Days sits in judgment, and “one like a son of man” comes with the clouds of heaven to the Ancient of Days and receives everlasting dominion (7:13-14). The kingdom is then given to the saints of the Most High (7:18, 22, 27).","keyIssue":"Who the little horn is (Antiochus IV, a Roman emperor, the papacy, or a future Antichrist) and what direction the Son of Man's “coming” takes. In 7:13 he comes to the Ancient of Days, which many read as Christ's ascension and enthronement; futurists read it as his return to earth. Jesus' use of the verse (Mark 14:62) is central.","timeText":false,"topics":["antichrist","kingdom","second-coming","great-tribulation","church-history"],"readings":[{"position":"postmil","reading":"The Son of Man ascends to the Ancient of Days (7:13), which is Christ's ascension and enthronement, and receives a kingdom in which all peoples, nations and languages will serve him (7:14). That service is achieved through the gospel in history. The saints share his rule (7:18, 27), which postmillennialists connect with the church's task of discipling the nations.","support":"The direction is explicit: the figure comes to the Ancient of Days, not down to earth. Jesus' post-resurrection claim to all authority (Matthew 28:18) echoes 7:14 in the Greek translation. The worldwide scope (all nations serving) shapes the postmillennial hope.","objection":"Critics note that the saints receive the kingdom only after the little horn's persecution and the judgment (7:21-22, 26-27), which places the full kingdom at the end, not in a gradual process within history.","sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","mathison-postmillennialism","wright-jesus-victory"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"7:13-14 is fulfilled in Christ's ascension and heavenly enthronement, and the saints receive the kingdom now in principle and at the end in fullness. The little horn is a type of the final Antichrist: an anti-God ruler who persecutes the church. Antiochus IV (pictured in Daniel 8) is an early instance, and a final figure will appear before the end. The “time, times and half a time” is symbolic of the limited period of the church's suffering, picked up in Revelation 12-13.","support":"The book of Revelation reuses Daniel 7 for the church age (the beast of Revelation 13 combines Daniel's four beasts), which suggests the pattern is ongoing. Jesus' words to the high priest (Mark 14:62) say the Sanhedrin will see him enthroned, which fits ascension.","objection":"Dispensationalists argue that the little horn's specific actions and final destruction at the judgment (7:11, 26) describe one individual at the end, not a recurring type. Preterists argue that the ruler should be found in the first century.","sources":["riddlebarger-man-of-sin-book","young-prophecy-of-daniel","beale-revelation-nigtc"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"The fourth beast is Rome and its later forms; the little horn is the future Antichrist, who persecutes the saints (the church) during the final tribulation. The Son of Man's receiving of the kingdom is enthronement, but its outworking on earth, when the saints take the kingdom (7:22), comes at Christ's return, followed by his earthly reign.","support":"The saints are persecuted by the horn until the judgment (7:21-22), which suggests that the church goes through the final tribulation, a key posttribulational point. Jesus in Matthew 24:30 uses Daniel 7:13 language for a future coming.","objection":"Amillennialists say that Daniel's kingdom is eternal (7:14, 27), with no intermediate millennial stage. Preterists say that Jesus applied Daniel 7:13 to events within that generation.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","grudem-systematic"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"The ten horns are a future ten-nation confederacy arising from a revived Roman empire. The little horn is the coming Antichrist, who will rule it and persecute Israel and tribulation saints for three and a half years (the second half of Daniel's seventieth week). The Son of Man then comes in glory to destroy him and establish the millennial kingdom, in which the saints reign.","support":"Daniel 7, Daniel 9:27, 2 Thessalonians 2:3-8 and Revelation 13 describe a single blasphemous persecutor who rules for the same period (three and a half years, 42 months, 1260 days), which suggests one future person.","objection":"Critics argue that the dispensational scheme requires an unmentioned gap of millennia within the fourth beast. Many also reject reading the “coming” of 7:13 as a descent when the text describes an ascent to God's throne.","sources":["walvoord-daniel","pentecost-things-to-come"]},{"position":"historicism","reading":"The little horn is the papacy, rising among the ten kingdoms that succeeded Rome, uprooting three of them (in one common Protestant reckoning the Heruli, Vandals and Ostrogoths; Newton proposed a different three), speaking great words and persecuting the saints. By the year-day principle, its “time, times and half a time” is 1,260 years, often dated from AD 538 to 1798, when Napoleon's general took the pope prisoner. This was the dominant Protestant view from the Reformation into the nineteenth century.","support":"Historicists point to the long reign of papal power, its claims to change “times and law” (7:25), and the striking end of its political power in 1798. They note that the Reformers, the Westminster divines and Newton held this reading.","objection":"Critics argue that different historicists have assigned different start and end dates, and that the year-day principle is not a stated rule of prophecy. Roman Catholic and many Protestant scholars also reject the identification as polemical.","sources":["newton-observations-daniel","elliott-horae","barnes-notes-revelation"]},{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"The fourth beast is Rome; the little horn is a first-century persecuting ruler (proposals vary, including Nero or the Flavian emperors). The Son of Man came to the Ancient of Days in the ascension, and his kingdom was publicly vindicated in AD 70, when the saints “possessed the kingdom” (7:22) as the old covenant order ended.","support":"Jesus applies Daniel 7:13 to what the Sanhedrin would see (Mark 14:62) and to the coming within his generation (Matthew 24:30, 34). Placing the vision in the Roman era fits the four-empire sequence.","objection":"Critics argue that no single first-century emperor clearly matches all the horn's details, and that proposals vary widely among preterists. Others argue that Daniel 7 should be read alongside chapters 8 and 11, which point to Antiochus.","sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","wright-jesus-victory"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"Daniel 7 was entirely fulfilled by AD 70: the Son of Man received his kingdom, the persecuting powers were judged, and the saints took possession of the everlasting kingdom when the old covenant ended. Nothing in the vision remains to be fulfilled.","support":"Full preterists link 7:22 (the time came for the saints to possess the kingdom) with Jesus' promise that the kingdom would come in power within the lifetime of his hearers (Mark 9:1).","objection":"Critics point out that Daniel 7 describes the destruction of all opposing kingdoms and the universal service of all peoples, which did not occur in AD 70. They note that the view rejects the creedal hope of a future return.","sources":["king-spirit-of-prophecy","mathison-when-shall"]}],"sources":["young-prophecy-of-daniel","walvoord-daniel","newton-observations-daniel","wright-jesus-victory"]},{"id":"daniel-9","title":"The Seventy Weeks","ref":"Daniel 9:24–27","ranges":["Daniel 9:24-27"],"category":"daniel","summary":"Gabriel tells Daniel that seventy “sevens” (shavuʿim) are decreed for his people and city: to finish transgression, put an end to sin, atone for iniquity, bring in everlasting righteousness, seal vision and prophet, and anoint a most holy place (or one). From a decree to rebuild Jerusalem to an anointed one are seven sevens and sixty-two sevens; after the sixty-two, an anointed one is “cut off”, and the people of a coming prince destroy the city and sanctuary. “He” confirms a covenant with many for one seven, and in its middle puts an end to sacrifice.","keyIssue":"Whether the seventy weeks run continuously, with the seventieth fulfilled in Christ's ministry and death (most preterist, postmillennial, amillennial and historicist readings), or whether there is a gap after the sixty-ninth, with the seventieth a future seven-year tribulation (dispensational). The crux is the antecedent of “he” in 9:27 (Messiah, or the coming prince) and which decree starts the count (538, 458 or 445/444 BC).","timeText":true,"topics":["seventy-weeks","great-tribulation","antichrist","israel"],"readings":[{"position":"postmil","reading":"The seventy weeks run continuously. Counting from Artaxerxes' decree to Ezra (458/457 BC), 483 years reach AD 26/27 and Jesus' baptism. In the middle of the seventieth week the Messiah is cut off, and his once-for-all sacrifice ends the validity of temple sacrifices. He “confirms the covenant with many” (the new covenant, compare Matthew 26:28). The destruction of AD 70 is the consequence of his rejection. The six goals of 9:24 are accomplished by Christ's atoning work.","support":"The six goals of 9:24 are redemptive (atonement, everlasting righteousness) and fit the cross. Nothing in the text signals an interruption of the count. The most natural antecedent of “he” is the Messiah, the main subject of the prophecy, and “confirming a covenant with many” echoes Isaiah 53:11-12.","objection":"Dispensationalists argue that the nearest antecedent of “he” is “the prince who is to come” (9:26), and that the destruction of the city in 9:26 falls after the sixty-nine weeks but is not placed in the seventieth, implying a gap. They also note that the 458 BC decree is about the temple and worship rather than rebuilding the city.","sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","mathison-postmillennialism","mauro-seventy-weeks"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"Most Reformed amillennialists also see the prophecy fulfilled in Christ. E. J. Young took the weeks as symbolic periods culminating in Christ; Meredith Kline argued that the Messiah confirms the covenant and that the seventieth week extends symbolically through the church age, so the second half corresponds to the church's tribulation (the 1,260 days of Revelation 11-12). Riddlebarger is broadly sympathetic to Kline's reading.","support":"Revelation reuses the half-week period (“time, times and half a time”, 42 months) for the church's experience between the advents, which suggests that the second half of the seventieth week is symbolically extended. The messianic reading of 9:27 has a long history in the church.","objection":"Dispensationalists argue that a symbolic stretching of the seventieth week is itself a kind of gap. Postmillennial preterists argue that the plain arithmetic fits Christ's ministry without extending the week.","sources":["young-prophecy-of-daniel","kline-seventieth-week","riddlebarger-case-amil"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"Historic premillennialists are divided. Many accept the messianic reading of 9:25-26 (Christ's coming and death) while leaving open a final fulfilment of 9:27's desolator in the end-time Antichrist, as Jesus' use of the abomination in Matthew 24:15 and Paul's man of lawlessness (2 Thessalonians 2:4) may suggest. Others adopt a futurist seventieth week without the full dispensational framework.","support":"Jesus treats the abomination of desolation as still future in his day (Matthew 24:15), which seems to place at least part of Daniel 9:27 after the crucifixion. Early church writers such as Irenaeus and Hippolytus already saw a future Antichrist in the last week.","objection":"Preterists answer that Jesus' “abomination” was fulfilled within his generation in AD 70, and dispensationalists answer that the divided historic premillennial approach lacks a coherent account of the gap.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","irenaeus-against-heresies"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"Sixty-nine weeks (483 years of 360 days each) run from Artaxerxes' decree to Nehemiah (444 BC) to Christ's triumphal entry (Hoehner: AD 33). Messiah is cut off after the sixty-ninth week; then the prophetic clock stops for the church age. The seventieth week is a future seven-year tribulation that begins when the coming prince (the Antichrist, from the people who destroyed the temple, the Romans) makes a covenant with Israel; he breaks it in the middle by stopping temple sacrifices.","support":"The events of 9:26 (the cutting off and the destruction of the city) come “after” the sixty-two weeks, not in the seventieth, which, Ice argues, implies a gap. The six goals of 9:24 include Israel's national restoration, which has not happened. Jesus placed the abomination in the future (Matthew 24:15).","objection":"Critics say a 2,000-year gap within a counted period of seventy weeks would make the counting meaningless, and that the antecedent of “he” in 9:27 is more naturally the Messiah, since “the prince” in 9:26 is subordinate to “the people”. Hoehner's 360-day year calculation is also disputed.","sources":["ice-why-gap-daniel-70-weeks","hoehner-chronological-aspects","walvoord-daniel"]},{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"The seventy weeks were completely fulfilled in the first century. Christ's work accomplished the goals of 9:24; he was cut off in the middle of the seventieth week; and the desolation of 9:26-27 is the Roman destruction of Jerusalem, which Jesus explicitly linked to Daniel (Matthew 24:15). Some (Gentry) end the seventieth week around AD 34 with the gospel turning to the Gentiles; AD 70 is the outworking of the covenant judgment.","support":"Jesus' warning about the abomination (Matthew 24:15) and Luke's parallel (Luke 21:20) tie Daniel's desolation to the Roman siege. A continuous count fits the arithmetic and the theological goals.","objection":"Critics observe that AD 70 falls nearly forty years after the end of the seventieth week on this reading, so 9:26-27 must refer to events outside the seventy weeks, which they see as a gap of another kind.","sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","mauro-seventy-weeks","demar-last-days-madness"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"The seventieth week extends to AD 70 and includes the consummation of all prophecy. The goals of 9:24 (to seal vision and prophecy, bring in everlasting righteousness) were completed at the destruction of the temple, so all prophecy, including the resurrection, was fulfilled then.","support":"Full preterists argue that “sealing up vision and prophet” means the fulfilment of all prophecy, which places every remaining expectation, including the resurrection, within the seventy weeks.","objection":"Critics argue that this stretches the seventieth week to about forty years, which is no more natural than the dispensational gap, and that the claim that all prophecy has been fulfilled conflicts with the creedal confession of a future return and resurrection.","sources":["king-spirit-of-prophecy","mathison-when-shall"]},{"position":"historicism","reading":"Classical historicists (Newton, Barnes, Elliott) read the seventy weeks as fulfilled in Christ: counting from Artaxerxes' seventh year (457 BC), 483 years reach AD 27 and the Messiah's public ministry. They regarded this prophecy as the clearest confirmation of the year-day principle, since its “weeks” must be weeks of years.","support":"The accuracy of the count in years, starting from a known decree and ending in Christ's ministry, is offered as a template for interpreting other prophetic periods in years.","objection":"Critics accept that the seventy weeks are “sevens” of years but deny that this justifies applying a day-for-a-year rule to the other numbers in Daniel and Revelation, where the text speaks of days and months.","sources":["newton-observations-daniel","barnes-notes-revelation"]}],"sources":["ice-why-gap-daniel-70-weeks","young-prophecy-of-daniel","hoehner-chronological-aspects","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"]},{"id":"2-thessalonians-2","title":"The Man of Lawlessness","ref":"2 Thessalonians 2:1–12","ranges":["2 Thessalonians 2:1-12"],"category":"epistles","summary":"Concerning the coming (parousia) of Christ and our gathering to him, Paul tells the Thessalonians not to be shaken by claims that the Day of the Lord has already come. It will not come unless the apostasia (rebellion or apostasy) comes first and the man of lawlessness is revealed, who sets himself up in the temple of God. Something (neuter, to katechon, 2:6) and someone (masculine, ho katechōn, 2:7) restrains him now; the mystery of lawlessness is “already at work”. When the restrainer is removed, the lawless one will be revealed, and the Lord Jesus will destroy him with the breath of his mouth and the appearance of his coming.","keyIssue":"Who the restrainer is, what the apostasia is, and whether the man of lawlessness is a first-century figure (Nero, or a Jewish leader in the AD 70 crisis), the papacy, or a future Antichrist. Also contested is which coming is in view: the parousia that destroys him (2:8) could be the final return or, on preterist readings, the AD 70 judgment.","timeText":true,"topics":["antichrist","rapture","second-coming","great-tribulation"],"readings":[{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"Gentry reads 2 Thessalonians 2 as concerned with first-century events. The man of lawlessness is Nero; the restrainer is Claudius and Roman law (noting the claudere, “to shut up”, wordplay), which held Nero back until his accession in AD 54. The temple is the Jerusalem temple; the apostasy is the Jewish revolt against Rome and against Christ. The “coming” is Christ's judgment-coming in AD 70. Warfield (1886) similarly read the man of sin as the line of Roman emperors.","support":"Paul's readers knew who the restrainer was (2:5-6) and the mystery was “already at work” (2:7), which suggests a first-century referent. A standing Jerusalem temple existed when Paul wrote, and Nero's self-deifying claims and his persecution are well documented.","objection":"Critics argue that Nero never set himself up in the Jerusalem temple, and that the one who is destroyed by Christ's coming (2:8) is linked to the parousia of 2:1, which includes the believers' “gathering” to Christ, a final, future event. Many partial preterists themselves hold the passage is about the end.","sources":["gentry-restrainer-man-of-lawlessness","gentry-before-jerusalem-fell"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"Before Christ returns there will be a great apostasy within the visible church, and a final Antichrist will appear. The spirit of lawlessness is at work in every age (2:7; 1 John 2:18), and it culminates in a person. The “temple of God” may be the church (Paul's normal use of naos in 1 Corinthians 3:16 and 2 Corinthians 6:16) where false religion takes over. Amillennialists vary on the restrainer: the preaching of the gospel, God's providential order, or civil government.","support":"Paul joins the parousia with the believers' gathering (2:1) and with the destruction of the lawless one (2:8), so all three happen at the one return. Paul speaks of the mystery already working, which fits a recurring pattern with a final climax.","objection":"Preterists argue that Paul's readers would have understood the temple as the actual Jerusalem temple, and dispensationalists argue that a spiritualised temple weakens the force of the passage.","sources":["riddlebarger-man-of-sin-podcast","riddlebarger-man-of-sin-book","vos-pauline-eschatology","hoekema-bible-future"]},{"position":"postmil","reading":"Many postmillennialists (Gentry, and Warfield before him) take the partial-preterist view, so the passage does not predict an apostasy at the end of the church age and does not undercut a hopeful view of history. Others, including older postmillennialists, place the apostasy and man of sin later in history (the papacy, for many Puritans) or allow for the final rebellion of Revelation 20:7-9 before the return.","support":"If the passage was fulfilled in the first century, it cannot prove that history ends in global apostasy, which removes a major objection to postmillennial optimism.","objection":"Amillennialists answer that the passage most naturally describes the time immediately before the final return, so its warning of apostasy contradicts the expectation of a Christianised world at the end.","sources":["gentry-restrainer-man-of-lawlessness","warfield-millennium-apocalypse"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"The passage is strong evidence for a posttribulational rapture: Paul tells the church that the “gathering to him” (2:1) will not happen before the apostasy and the revelation of the man of lawlessness. The church will see the Antichrist, and Christ will destroy him at his coming. The restrainer is often identified as God's providential order (Roman law in Paul's day) rather than the church.","support":"If believers were to be removed before the Antichrist appeared, Paul's simplest answer to the Thessalonians' fear would have been “you will be gone before then”. Instead he gives signs they will see first.","objection":"Pretribulationists argue that Paul's point is that the Day of the Lord (the tribulation) has not begun, which says nothing about the timing of the rapture itself. They also read the restrainer's removal as the church's removal.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","gundry-church-tribulation","hultberg-three-views-rapture"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"The Thessalonians feared they were already in the Day of the Lord, the tribulation. Paul reassures them that it cannot begin until the restrainer, the Holy Spirit working through the church, is taken out of the way (ek mesou genētai) at the rapture. Some pretribulationists (E. Schuyler English, H. Wayne House, Thomas Ice) render apostasia as “departure”, meaning the rapture itself. The man of lawlessness is the future Antichrist, who will sit in a rebuilt Jerusalem temple.","support":"Only a divine power could restrain Satan's working, and the neuter/masculine switch fits the Holy Spirit (pneuma is neuter in Greek, but the Spirit is a person). The comfort Paul gives (1 Thessalonians 5:9; 2 Thessalonians 2:13) suggests believers are not appointed to the wrath of that day.","objection":"Critics note that the text does not name the Spirit, that apostasia in the New Testament and the Greek Old Testament means rebellion or defection, not spatial departure (Acts 21:21), and that the Spirit could not be removed from the world while people are still being saved during the tribulation.","sources":["walvoord-rapture-question","pentecost-things-to-come","hultberg-three-views-rapture"]},{"position":"historicism","reading":"The man of sin is the papacy, a line of persons in an office, which sits in the church (the temple of God) claiming divine prerogatives. The restrainer was the pagan Roman empire, whose fall cleared the way for papal power. This reading is in the original Westminster Confession (25.6) and was common among the Reformers.","support":"Historicists argue that the description of an apostasy within the church, centred in the “temple of God”, with a claim to sit in God's place, fits the papacy's development after Rome's fall better than any individual.","objection":"Critics say the passage describes an individual (“the man”, “the son of destruction”) destroyed at Christ's coming, not an institution lasting many centuries; many later Reformed bodies (for example the PCUSA in 1903) removed the identification from their confessions.","sources":["westminster-confession","elliott-horae","barnes-notes-revelation"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"The coming of 2:1 and 2:8 is the parousia of AD 70. The man of lawlessness is a first-century figure (proposals include the Jewish high priestly establishment, Zealot leaders in the temple, or Nero), and the gathering of the saints and the destruction of the lawless one took place then.","support":"Full preterists argue that the parousia of 2:1 and 2:8 should be the same coming as Matthew 24, which Jesus placed within his generation.","objection":"Critics answer that the “gathering” language links this passage to 1 Thessalonians 4:17, a bodily resurrection, which did not occur in AD 70. Partial preterists add that identifying the coming of 2:8 with AD 70 is possible without denying a future bodily return.","sources":["king-spirit-of-prophecy","mathison-when-shall"]}],"sources":["bruce-thessalonians-wbc","gentry-restrainer-man-of-lawlessness","riddlebarger-man-of-sin-book","walvoord-rapture-question"]},{"id":"1-thessalonians-4-5","title":"The Coming of the Lord and the Day of the Lord","ref":"1 Thessalonians 4:13–5:11","ranges":["1 Thessalonians 4:13-18","1 Thessalonians 5:1-11"],"category":"epistles","summary":"Paul comforts grieving believers: at the coming (parousia) of the Lord, those who have died in Christ will rise first, then “we who are alive” will be caught up (harpagēsometha, Latin rapiemur, hence “rapture”) together with them in the clouds to meet (eis apantēsin) the Lord in the air. The Day of the Lord will come like a thief on the unprepared, but believers are children of the day and are not destined for wrath but for salvation.","keyIssue":"Whether this “catching up” is a separate, pretribulational coming for the church or the single visible return (and whether apantēsis, a civic term for a delegation going out to escort a visiting dignitary back to the city, implies the saints return with Christ to earth). Also, whether “we who are alive” is a time statement, and whether 5:1-11 describes the same event as 4:13-18.","timeText":true,"topics":["rapture","second-coming","first-resurrection","final-judgment"],"readings":[{"position":"disppremil","reading":"This is the rapture of the church, distinct from the Second Coming. Christ comes for his saints (in the air) before the seven-year tribulation, and later comes with them to earth (Zechariah 14:5; Revelation 19:14). The passage says nothing about tribulation signs, unlike Matthew 24, and the comfort Paul offers depends on the church being spared the Day of the Lord's wrath (5:9; 1:10). Prewrath advocates (Rosenthal) place the rapture later, inside the seventieth week but before God's wrath.","support":"The language of comfort (4:18), the absence of any warning about the Antichrist, and the promise of deliverance from wrath (5:9) fit a rapture before the tribulation. Differences from Matthew 24 (resurrection, meeting in the air, no signs) suggest distinct events.","objection":"Critics answer that wrath in 5:9 means final condemnation, not the tribulation, and that Paul uses the same terms (parousia, trumpet, angel, clouds) as Jesus in Matthew 24:30-31 for a coming after the tribulation. Nothing in the text separates this coming from the visible return.","sources":["walvoord-rapture-question","pentecost-things-to-come","rosenthal-prewrath"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"The rapture is the gathering of the church at Christ's single, visible, posttribulational return. Apantēsis describes a welcoming delegation that goes out to meet a visiting ruler and escorts him back, so believers rise to meet Christ and accompany him to earth to reign. The resurrection here is the first resurrection of Revelation 20:4-5, before the millennium.","support":"The same word (apantēsis) is used in Matthew 25:6 and Acts 28:15 of people going out to meet someone and returning with them. The loud, public signals (a cry of command, an archangel's voice, God's trumpet) are hard to fit a secret event.","objection":"Pretribulationists argue that apantēsis need not have its technical sense here, and that the passage does not say where Christ and the church go after the meeting. They also argue that posttribulationism makes the rapture an odd up-and-down event.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","gundry-church-tribulation","hultberg-three-views-rapture"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"This is the one future return of Christ, when all the dead in Christ are raised, living believers are transformed and caught up, and the final judgment and new creation follow. The Day of the Lord (5:2) is the same event from the side of the unbelieving world, which it overtakes with sudden destruction (5:3). There is no separate rapture and no intermediate millennium after it.","support":"Paul treats the parousia and the Day of the Lord as one event with two sides: rescue for believers, destruction for unbelievers (compare 2 Thessalonians 1:6-10). Other texts tie the resurrection of believers to “the last day” (John 6:39-40).","objection":"Premillennialists reply that Paul mentions only the resurrection of believers here, which leaves room for the resurrection of the rest at a later point (Revelation 20:5).","sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil","waldron-end-times-simple"]},{"position":"postmil","reading":"Postmillennialists read the passage as amillennialists do: one visible return at the end of history, a general resurrection and the final judgment. Paul's “we who are alive” expresses solidarity with the whole church, not a prediction that the return would come within his lifetime. Most postmillennial partial preterists (Gentry, Mathison) regard this passage as still future.","support":"Paul admits he does not know the time (5:1-2), and elsewhere he expects that he might die before the return (2 Corinthians 5:1-8; Philippians 1:21-23), so “we” is a general self-inclusion.","objection":"Full preterists argue that partial preterists are inconsistent: they accept the time texts of Matthew 24 as fulfilled in AD 70 but not Paul's “we who are alive”.","sources":["mathison-when-shall","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"The parousia and gathering were fulfilled in AD 70. Paul expected the coming in his own generation (“we who are alive”), and it came as the spiritual and covenantal resurrection of God's people and their entrance into the presence of God, not a physical removal of bodies. The “Day of the Lord” was the destruction of Jerusalem.","support":"Full preterists treat “we who are alive and remain” as a plain time statement, and link the trumpet and gathering to Matthew 24:31, which Jesus placed within his generation.","objection":"Critics argue that the text speaks of the dead rising and the living being caught up, which no one observed in AD 70, and that this reading denies the bodily resurrection confessed by all the historic creeds. Proponents answer that the resurrection is a covenantal, not biological, event.","sources":["preston-we-shall-meet-him","king-spirit-of-prophecy","mathison-when-shall"]},{"position":"futurism","reading":"All futurists place this event at the end of the age, but they divide on its timing relative to a future tribulation: pretribulational (before the seventieth week), midtribulational, prewrath (after the Antichrist's persecution but before God's wrath, after the sixth seal), and posttribulational (at the single return).","support":"Each timing view reads 5:9 (not appointed to wrath) and the parallels with Matthew 24 and 2 Thessalonians 2 differently; the question is how much of the future tribulation counts as divine wrath.","objection":"Non-futurists argue the debate rests on a future seven-year tribulation that the text never mentions.","sources":["hultberg-three-views-rapture","rosenthal-prewrath"]}],"sources":["hultberg-three-views-rapture","bruce-thessalonians-wbc","ladd-blessed-hope","walvoord-rapture-question"]},{"id":"1-corinthians-15-20-28","title":"Each in His Own Order","ref":"1 Corinthians 15:20–28","ranges":["1 Corinthians 15:20-28"],"category":"epistles","summary":"Christ is the firstfruits (aparchē) of the resurrection; then (epeita) those who belong to Christ at his coming (parousia); then (eita) comes the end (to telos), when he hands over the kingdom to the Father after destroying every rule and power. He must reign until (achri hou) God has put all enemies under his feet (Psalm 110:1); the last enemy to be destroyed is death.","keyIssue":"Whether there is a long interval between “then at his coming” and “then the end” (a millennium after the return), or whether the end comes at the parousia. Closely connected: when Christ's reign of 15:25 takes place. If it is now, at the Father's right hand, and ends at the return, there is no room for a later millennial reign; it also matters whether enemies are subdued before he returns.","timeText":false,"topics":["millennium","kingdom","second-coming","optimism","final-judgment"],"readings":[{"position":"postmil","reading":"Christ is reigning now from his ascension, and that reign must continue until all his enemies are subdued. Paul draws on Psalm 110:1: the session at the Father's right hand lasts until the enemies are a footstool. Every rule and authority is defeated during the present age, through the gospel, and only the last enemy, death, is destroyed at his return. Christ's parousia brings the end, not a further era.","support":"Paul places the subduing of enemies within the reign that precedes the end, and he applies Psalm 110:1 to Christ's present session (as do Acts 2:34-35 and Hebrews 10:12-13). Death is abolished at the resurrection (15:54-55), so the return coincides with the end.","objection":"Amillennialists agree on the timing but argue that the subduing of enemies is decisively accomplished at the cross and finally at the return, not progressively in history. Premillennialists argue that “then the end” leaves room for a period after the return.","sources":["gentry-postmil-1-corinthians-15","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","bahnsen-victory-in-jesus"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"There are two stages in the resurrection: Christ, then his people at his coming. The end follows at that coming, with no millennium in between. Christ's reign (15:25) is his present heavenly reign, which lasts until the parousia, when death, the last enemy, is destroyed in the resurrection. A millennium after the return would mean that death continues after the resurrection, which Paul's sequence excludes.","support":"In 15:54-55 the resurrection at the last trumpet is when “death is swallowed up in victory”. If death is destroyed at the return, there can be no subsequent age in which people still die (as premillennialists usually hold, compare Isaiah 65:20).","objection":"Premillennialists argue that eita (“then”) in 15:24 can indicate a gap, just as epeita in 15:23 covers the interval of nearly two thousand years between Christ's resurrection and the parousia.","sources":["vos-pauline-eschatology","hoekema-bible-future","waldron-end-times-simple"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"Paul lists ordered stages: Christ the firstfruits, then believers at his coming, then the end. Since the first two are separated by a long period, the last two may be also, which makes room for the millennial reign of Revelation 20 between the parousia and the end. Ladd and others regard this as the clearest Pauline hint of a millennium.","support":"The words epeita and eita both indicate succession, and the first interval is already about two thousand years. The final handing over of the kingdom to the Father (15:24) presupposes a period of the Son's rule.","objection":"Amillennialists and postmillennialists answer that the reign of 15:25 is located before the end by Psalm 110:1, which describes the present session, and that death being destroyed at the parousia excludes a later period in which death still operates.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","blomberg-chung-historic-premil","grudem-systematic"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"Christ's reign in 15:25 includes his millennial reign on David's throne on earth after his return. The present session at the Father's right hand (Revelation 3:21 distinguishes “my throne” and “my Father's throne”) is not yet the Davidic reign, which begins at the Second Coming and ends when he hands the kingdom over after the millennium.","support":"Distinguishing the Father's throne from Christ's own throne (Revelation 3:21; Matthew 25:31) suggests a future throne on earth. A gap between epeita and eita is possible on grammatical grounds.","objection":"Critics argue that Acts 2:30-36 places Christ on David's throne at his ascension (a point accepted by progressive dispensationalists such as Bock), and that the destruction of death at the parousia leaves no room for death during a later millennium.","sources":["vlach-he-will-reign-forever","walvoord-millennial-kingdom","blaising-bock-progressive"]},{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"Partial preterists, whether postmillennial or amillennial, regard this passage as describing a still-future bodily resurrection at Christ's final return, and treat it as the clearest refutation of full preterism. The parousia here is the coming that raises the dead and destroys death, which did not happen in AD 70.","support":"The resurrection in 15:20-23 is modelled on Christ's own bodily resurrection (firstfruits of the same harvest), and death as the last enemy refers to physical death, as Paul's whole argument against those denying bodily resurrection (15:12) shows.","objection":"Full preterists answer that the context (15:56, “the power of sin is the law”) shows that the death in view is covenantal death under the law, which was abolished when the old covenant ended in AD 70.","sources":["mathison-when-shall","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"The resurrection is corporate and covenantal: the “body” of God's people is raised from the death of the old Adamic and Mosaic covenant order into the life of the new covenant. That was completed at the parousia of AD 70, when Christ finished subduing his enemies and death, whose power was the law (15:56), was conquered. Max King's “covenant eschatology” develops this.","support":"Proponents point to 15:56, which connects death's sting to sin and the law, and to the singular “body” (sōma) of 15:44, which they read corporately.","objection":"Critics argue that Paul's whole argument concerns individual bodily resurrection modelled on Christ's empty tomb (15:3-8, 12-20), that the creeds universally confess a future bodily resurrection, and that this view therefore lies outside historic Christian orthodoxy.","sources":["king-cross-parousia","mathison-when-shall"]}],"sources":["vos-pauline-eschatology","ladd-blessed-hope","gentry-postmil-1-corinthians-15","mathison-when-shall"]},{"id":"romans-11","title":"All Israel Will Be Saved","ref":"Romans 11:25–27","ranges":["Romans 11:11-32"],"category":"epistles","summary":"Paul reveals a mystery: a partial hardening (pōrōsis apo merous) has come upon Israel until (achri hou) the fullness (plērōma) of the Gentiles has come in, “and so” (kai houtōs) all Israel will be saved, as written in Isaiah 59:20-21 (the Deliverer will come from Zion). The broader passage (11:11-32) speaks of Israel's “fullness” bringing much greater blessing to the world (11:12) and of their acceptance meaning “life from the dead” (11:15). The gifts and calling of God are irrevocable (11:29).","keyIssue":"What “all Israel” means: the whole church of Jews and Gentiles, the total of elect Jews across history, or the ethnic nation at a future point. Also: whether houtōs means “in this way” or (as many read it) implies a time sequence, and whether the coming of the Deliverer is the first or the second advent.","timeText":false,"topics":["israel","optimism","second-coming","church-history"],"readings":[{"position":"postmil","reading":"After the full number of Gentiles come in, there will be a widespread conversion of ethnic Jews to Christ, within history, before the end. That conversion will in turn bring great blessing to the world, “life from the dead” (11:15). This expectation fuelled the Puritan and evangelical missions movement (Edwards, the Puritans in Murray's study, Hodge).","support":"Paul contrasts Israel's present partial hardening (“until”) with a future change, and promises that Israel's full inclusion will bring much greater blessing (11:12, 15), which suggests a historical sequence with further worldwide effects.","objection":"Critics say “life from the dead” may well refer to the final resurrection, which would place Israel's conversion at the end rather than before a further period of blessing. Others deny that the passage predicts a mass conversion at all.","sources":["murray-puritan-hope","edwards-history-redemption","boettner-millennium"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"Amillennialists are divided. Hoekema (and Merkle, Robertson, Berkhof) argues that “all Israel” means the total number of elect Jews throughout the age, gathered alongside Gentiles; “and so” refers to the manner of their salvation. Calvin read “all Israel” as the whole people of God. Others, such as Riddlebarger and Vos, expect a future large-scale conversion of Jews near the end, before the return.","support":"Houtōs normally means “in this manner”, not “then”. Romans 9:6 distinguishes two senses of Israel, and 11:5 describes a present remnant, which suggests a continuing process rather than a single future event. The present tense of 11:31 (“that they now may receive mercy”) points to the present age.","objection":"Critics argue that in 11:25 “Israel” plainly means ethnic Israel, as distinct from Gentiles, and that a future conversion is the most natural reading of the contrast between present hardening and future salvation.","sources":["hoekema-bible-future","merkle-romans-11","riddlebarger-case-amil"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"At or near Christ's return, the Deliverer coming from Zion, there will be a large-scale turning of ethnic Israel to Christ. Historic premillennialists hold that Israel and the church become one people in Christ, but that God has a future for the Jewish people, who will enter the millennial kingdom.","support":"Paul quotes Isaiah 59:20, which can be read of the Second Coming, and Moo and others regard a large end-time conversion of ethnic Israel as the most natural reading.","objection":"Others reply that Paul's quotation may describe Christ's first coming and the removal of sin through the new covenant (Jeremiah 31:33-34), not a future event.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","moo-romans-nicnt","blomberg-chung-historic-premil"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"The passage is a key text for God's distinct future for national Israel. After the church age, the time of the Gentiles' fullness, national Israel will repent and turn to its Messiah at the Second Coming (compare Zechariah 12:10). This will lead to the fulfilment of the land and kingdom promises in the millennium, since the gifts and calling of God are irrevocable (11:29).","support":"Paul distinguishes Israel and the Gentiles throughout Romans 11, and irrevocable promises (11:28-29) seem to include the national and territorial covenants. The olive tree image (11:17-24) assumes natural branches can be grafted back.","objection":"Critics argue that Paul speaks of salvation, forgiveness and inclusion in the olive tree with Gentiles, not territorial promises or a separate future programme for Israel.","sources":["vlach-he-will-reign-forever","walvoord-millennial-kingdom","ryrie-dispensationalism"]},{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"Partial preterists vary. Many, following postmillennial tradition, expect a future conversion of the Jews. Some see the context in the first-century mission and the AD 70 crisis, with N. T. Wright (often grouped with preterist readings of the Gospels) arguing that “all Israel” is the whole people of God, Jew and Gentile, completed through the gospel.","support":"Wright points to Paul's redefinition of Israel around Christ (Romans 2:28-29; 9:6; Galatians 6:16), and to the quotation of Isaiah 59 with “from Zion” rather than “for Zion”, suggesting the Deliverer goes out from Zion to the nations.","objection":"Critics argue that the shift from “Israel” meaning ethnic Jews in 11:25 to “all Israel” meaning the church in 11:26 is abrupt and unlikely within the same sentence.","sources":["wright-climax-covenant","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"]},{"position":"progressive-covenantalism","reading":"Progressive covenantalists (Wellum, Gentry) hold that Christ is the true Israel and that the land promises are fulfilled typologically in the new creation. Many still allow, with Moo and Schreiner, that Romans 11 predicts a future large-scale salvation of ethnic Jews, who are joined to the one people of God rather than given a separate national programme.","support":"This reading accepts a future for ethnic Jews while treating the covenants as fulfilled in Christ, which they argue fits both Romans 11 and Ephesians 2:11-22.","objection":"Dispensationalists argue that it promises Israel salvation but not the specific national restoration of the prophets. Covenant theologians argue that it adds little to the Reformed amillennial or postmillennial view.","sources":["gentry-wellum-kingdom-covenant","moo-romans-nicnt"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"The salvation of all Israel was completed at the parousia of AD 70, when the Deliverer came out of (heavenly) Zion and the covenant promises were fulfilled. “All Israel” is the faithful remnant together with believing Gentiles.","support":"Full preterists connect the quotation of Isaiah 59:20 to the coming in judgment and salvation that Jesus placed within his generation.","objection":"Critics say there is no evidence of a large-scale salvation of Jews in AD 70; on the contrary, the destruction of Jerusalem was a judgment on unbelief.","sources":["king-spirit-of-prophecy","mathison-when-shall"]}],"sources":["merkle-romans-11","moo-romans-nicnt","murray-puritan-hope","hoekema-bible-future"]},{"id":"psalm-2","title":"The Lord's Anointed","ref":"Psalm 2","ranges":["Psalms 2"],"category":"psalms","summary":"The nations rage and their rulers plot against the LORD and his anointed. God laughs, having installed his king on Zion. The king announces God's decree, “You are my Son; today I have begotten you”, and the promise of the nations as his inheritance, to be broken with a rod of iron. The kings are warned to serve the LORD and “kiss the Son”.","keyIssue":"When the Son receives the nations and rules them with the rod. The New Testament applies 2:1-2 to the crucifixion (Acts 4:25-27) and 2:7 to the resurrection (Acts 13:33; Hebrews 1:5), but Revelation applies the rod of iron to Christ's return and to the reign of the saints (Revelation 2:26-27; 12:5; 19:15).","timeText":false,"topics":["kingdom","optimism","second-coming","millennium"],"readings":[{"position":"postmil","reading":"The Son was installed as king at his resurrection and ascension and is now claiming the nations given to him as an inheritance (2:8) through the preaching of the gospel. The psalm summons rulers to submit to Christ in history (2:10-12), which postmillennialists see as a mandate and a promise that nations, not just individuals, will be discipled.","support":"Acts 13:33 places the decree of 2:7 at the resurrection, so the inheritance is offered now. The call to kings to serve the LORD is addressed to rulers within history, before judgment.","objection":"Critics answer that the rod of iron in Revelation 19:15 is applied to Christ's return, which shows that the decisive subjugation of the nations is at the end, not progressively in history.","sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","bahnsen-victory-in-jesus"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"Christ was enthroned on the heavenly Zion at his resurrection and ascension (Hebrews 12:22) and reigns now. The nations' rebellion continues throughout this age, and the rod of iron describes his final judgment at the return, as well as the present triumph of his people (Revelation 2:26-27).","support":"The New Testament applies the psalm to the crucifixion, resurrection and exaltation, which places the king's installation in the past and the judgment in the future, without an intervening millennium.","objection":"Postmillennialists argue that this underplays the promise of the nations as an inheritance; premillennialists argue that it removes the earthly dimension of the rule from Zion.","sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"Christ was declared Son at the resurrection, but his rule over the nations with a rod of iron awaits his return and the millennial kingdom, in which the saints share it (Revelation 2:26-27). The psalm thus spans the first and second advents.","support":"Revelation 2:26-27 promises the overcomers authority over the nations with an iron rod, which premillennialists read as their reign in the millennium after the return (Revelation 20:4).","objection":"Amillennialists argue that Revelation 2:27 describes the saints sharing Christ's victory, already begun, not a future earthly reign.","sources":["ladd-commentary-revelation","grudem-systematic"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"Classical dispensationalists hold that Christ is not yet reigning on David's throne on Zion; the installation and rod of iron are fulfilled at the Second Coming, when he rules the nations from Jerusalem in the millennium. Progressive dispensationalists (Bock, Blaising) argue that the Davidic reign was inaugurated at the resurrection (Acts 2; 13), with the earthly rule from Zion still future.","support":"The psalm speaks of an earthly Zion and of crushing the rebellious nations, which dispensationalists argue has not happened yet.","objection":"Critics say the New Testament's own use of the psalm (Acts 4:25-27; 13:33) places the installation at the resurrection, and Hebrews 12:22 identifies the Zion of the new covenant as heavenly.","sources":["vlach-he-will-reign-forever","blaising-bock-progressive","walvoord-millennial-kingdom"]},{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"The rulers raging against the anointed include Herod, Pilate and the leaders of Israel (Acts 4:25-27). The warning of 2:12 (that his wrath is quickly kindled) was carried out against unbelieving Jerusalem in AD 70, and the Son continues to receive the nations in the gospel age.","support":"Acts 4 explicitly applies 2:1-2 to the first-century authorities, and Jesus warned those same authorities of imminent judgment (Matthew 23:36).","objection":"Critics say the psalm's horizon is universal (all the nations, the ends of the earth), so AD 70 is at most a partial instance, not the fulfilment.","sources":["demar-last-days-madness","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"]}],"sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","vlach-he-will-reign-forever","hoekema-bible-future"]},{"id":"psalm-110","title":"Sit at My Right Hand","ref":"Psalm 110","ranges":["Psalms 110"],"category":"psalms","summary":"The LORD says to David's Lord, “Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies your footstool.” The LORD will extend his mighty sceptre from Zion; he rules in the midst of his enemies; his people offer themselves willingly in the day of his power; he is a priest forever after the order of Melchizedek, and will shatter kings and judge among the nations. It is the Old Testament text most often quoted or alluded to in the New Testament.","keyIssue":"What happens during the session at the right hand. The New Testament places Christ's enthronement at his ascension (Acts 2:34-36; Hebrews 1:13; 10:12-13). The question is whether the subduing of enemies “until” the footstool is complete happens progressively during this age, all at once at the return, or after the return in a millennium.","timeText":false,"topics":["kingdom","optimism","millennium","second-coming"],"readings":[{"position":"postmil","reading":"Christ is now seated at the right hand and will remain there until his enemies are made his footstool. He rules “in the midst of his enemies” (110:2), and his people volunteer freely in the day of his power (110:3). The subjugation of enemies therefore happens in history, through the gospel, before he returns, and his return comes after that victory.","support":"Hebrews 10:12-13 pictures Christ seated, waiting until his enemies are made a footstool, which places their subjugation during the session. Paul uses the same psalm to describe the reign that ends in the end (1 Corinthians 15:25).","objection":"Amillennialists answer that the enemies are made a footstool in a final, decisive act at the return (compare 2 Thessalonians 1:7-9), not by gradual conversion of the world. Rule “in the midst of enemies” suggests ongoing opposition.","sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","bahnsen-victory-in-jesus","gentry-postmil-1-corinthians-15"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"Christ's session at the right hand is his present messianic reign, the millennium of Revelation 20. He rules now from the heavenly Zion through his word and Spirit, in the midst of enemies who will finally be subdued at his return and the last judgment. The psalm leaves no room for another reign after his return; at that point he hands the kingdom to the Father (1 Corinthians 15:24-28).","support":"Acts 2:34-36 announces Christ's enthronement as fulfilling the psalm, and Peter connects it with David's throne (Acts 2:30), which makes the present session the promised messianic reign.","objection":"Postmillennialists argue that this does not take seriously the progressive conquest implied in the psalm. Premillennialists argue that the session at the right hand is distinct from Christ's own kingdom on earth.","sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil","storms-kingdom-come"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"Christ reigns now at the right hand, which is a real inauguration of his kingdom. The subjugation of his enemies will be completed in stages: at his return he will judge the nations and reign on earth (110:5-6), and at the end of the millennium the last enemy, death, will be destroyed.","support":"The psalm moves from session (110:1) to judgment of kings and nations (110:5-6), which premillennialists see as a sequence beginning with the session and ending with a visible conquest, matching Revelation 19-20.","objection":"Critics argue that 1 Corinthians 15:25-26 places the destruction of death at the end of the session, so there is no period after the session in which death continues.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","blomberg-chung-historic-premil"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"Classical dispensationalists (Walvoord) distinguish the Father's throne, where Christ sits now as priest, from the Davidic throne he will occupy on earth after his return; the rule from Zion (110:2) is millennial. Progressive dispensationalists (Bock) accept that the Davidic reign was inaugurated at the ascension, according to Acts 2:30-36, but hold that its fulfilment on earth, in relation to Israel and the nations, is still future.","support":"Revelation 3:21 distinguishes Christ's throne from his Father's throne. The psalm's concrete judgment of kings and nations awaits his visible return.","objection":"Critics argue that Acts 2 applies the psalm to Christ's present reign as David's heir, which makes a separate future Davidic throne unnecessary.","sources":["walvoord-millennial-kingdom","blaising-bock-progressive","vlach-he-will-reign-forever"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"The session ended in AD 70, when his enemies (unbelieving Jerusalem and the old covenant order) were made his footstool and the day of his wrath (110:5) came. Christ reigns over a kingdom now fully established.","support":"Full preterists connect Jesus' warning to the high priest (Mark 14:62, citing Psalm 110:1 and Daniel 7:13) with the judgment within that generation.","objection":"Critics argue that death, the last enemy, has not been abolished, and that Paul places the subjugation of all enemies (including death) at the general resurrection.","sources":["king-spirit-of-prophecy","mathison-when-shall"]}],"sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","hoekema-bible-future","blaising-bock-progressive"]},{"id":"isaiah-2","title":"The Mountain of the LORD's House","ref":"Isaiah 2:2–4","ranges":["Isaiah 2:2-4"],"category":"prophets","summary":"“In the latter days” (beʾacharit hayyamim) the mountain of the LORD's house will be established as the highest of the mountains. All nations will stream to it to learn God's ways; instruction (torah) will go out from Zion; he will judge between the nations, and they will beat their swords into plowshares and learn war no more. Micah 4:1-3 has the same oracle.","keyIssue":"When the latter days are and what the mountain is. The New Testament says the last days began with Christ (Acts 2:17; Hebrews 1:2). The question is whether the oracle is fulfilled progressively in the church now, in a literal millennial Jerusalem after the return, or in the final new creation.","timeText":false,"topics":["kingdom","optimism","millennium","israel"],"readings":[{"position":"postmil","reading":"The latter days began with Christ's first coming. The mountain is the exalted kingdom of God, the heavenly Zion to which the church has come (Hebrews 12:22), and the nations are now streaming to it through the gospel. As this continues, the word of God will increasingly shape nations and international relations, bringing a real, if not perfect, era of peace before Christ returns.","support":"The New Testament places the latter days in the present (Acts 2:17; 1 Corinthians 10:11). The picture is of nations being taught and changed, not destroyed, which suits gradual conversion; judgment and peace come through instruction going out from Zion, which matches the Great Commission going out from Jerusalem (Luke 24:47).","objection":"Critics say the world has not seen a general end to war in the two millennia since, and amillennialists say the perfect peace described points to the new creation rather than to a stage in history.","sources":["boettner-millennium","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","murray-puritan-hope"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"The prophecy is fulfilled in the church (spiritual Zion) as people from all nations come to Christ now, and is consummated in the new heavens and new earth, where war is no more. It does not describe an intermediate millennial age with a rebuilt Jerusalem.","support":"Hebrews 12:22 identifies Zion with the heavenly city that believers have already come to, and Revelation 21-22 pictures the nations walking in the light of the city in the new creation.","objection":"Premillennialists say the oracle describes a period in which nations still need judging and arbitration (2:4), which does not fit the eternal state. Postmillennialists say amillennialism postpones its historical aspect too much.","sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil","motyer-prophecy-isaiah"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"The prophecy partly began with the gospel's spread, but its picture of universal peace among nations who still need arbitration (2:4) fits best the earthly reign of Christ after his return, before the eternal state.","support":"The oracle includes nations being judged and taught, which implies a world where sin is still present but restrained. That is what premillennialists expect in the millennium.","objection":"Amillennialists answer that the prophets regularly describe the eternal state with imagery from their own world, so arbitration between nations need not imply continuing sin.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","grudem-systematic"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"This is a literal prophecy of the millennial kingdom. Physical Mount Zion in Jerusalem will be elevated (compare Zechariah 14:10), the millennial temple will be there, and the nations will come to Jerusalem to be taught by the Messiah reigning on David's throne, while war ceases worldwide.","support":"The oracle uses specific geographic terms (Zion, Jerusalem, the house of the God of Jacob), and the companion passage Micah 4 adds that each will sit under his vine and fig tree, which implies an earthly, agricultural setting.","objection":"Critics argue that the New Testament reinterprets Zion and the temple in terms of Christ and the church (John 2:19-21; Hebrews 12:22), so a physical temple centre would reverse that fulfilment.","sources":["walvoord-millennial-kingdom","pentecost-things-to-come"]},{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"The “latter days” are the last days of the old covenant age, from Christ to AD 70 (Hebrews 1:2; 9:26). After the earthly temple was removed, the true house of the LORD, Christ and his church, was established as the chief mountain, and from then on the nations stream into it.","support":"The New Testament writers speak of living in the last days in their own time (Acts 2:16-17; 1 Peter 1:20), and the destruction of the old temple cleared the way for the new.","objection":"Critics argue that New Testament “last days” describe the whole period from Christ's first coming to his second, not only the forty years to AD 70.","sources":["demar-last-days-madness","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"]}],"sources":["motyer-prophecy-isaiah","boettner-millennium","walvoord-millennial-kingdom"]},{"id":"isaiah-9","title":"The Increase of His Government","ref":"Isaiah 9:6–7","ranges":["Isaiah 9:6-7"],"category":"prophets","summary":"A child is born and a son given, on whose shoulder the government rests; he is called Wonderful Counsellor, Mighty God (ʾel gibbor), Everlasting Father, Prince of Peace. “Of the increase (marbeh) of his government and of peace there will be no end”, on the throne of David, established with justice and righteousness forever. The zeal of the LORD of hosts will do this.","keyIssue":"When the child sits on David's throne and what “increase without end” means: continual growth of Christ's present reign through history, the eternal reign that begins at his return, or a literal Davidic throne in Jerusalem after the return. Matthew 4:15-16 applies Isaiah 9:1-2 to Jesus' Galilean ministry, and Luke 1:32-33 echoes 9:7.","timeText":false,"topics":["kingdom","optimism","millennium","israel"],"readings":[{"position":"postmil","reading":"The child is Jesus, who took up David's throne at his resurrection and ascension. His government will increase continually and without end, starting from small beginnings and growing through history, which is how postmillennialists read the progress of the gospel. The guarantee is the zeal of the LORD of hosts (9:7), not human effort.","support":"Marbeh speaks of increase or growth, not mere duration, which matches the kingdom parables of growth (Matthew 13:31-33). The throne of David is linked to Christ's resurrection and enthronement (Acts 2:30-31).","objection":"Critics say that “no end” may describe the permanence of the kingdom rather than continuous growth in history, and that the history of the church shows decline as well as growth.","sources":["boettner-millennium","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"This prophecy is fulfilled in Christ's present reign on David's throne at the Father's right hand, which expands as the gospel goes to the nations and lasts eternally. There is no separate future Davidic reign in Jerusalem; the eternal kingdom begun at the first advent is consummated at the return.","support":"The prophecy emphasises the eternal (“forever”), not a thousand-year reign, and the New Testament presents the risen Christ as already on David's throne (Acts 2:30-36; 13:34).","objection":"Premillennialists argue that the throne of David is a specific, earthly throne in Jerusalem, so heaven cannot be its location.","sources":["hoekema-bible-future","motyer-prophecy-isaiah"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"The child is Jesus, and his rule was inaugurated at his exaltation, but the full, visible reign of justice and peace on David's throne will appear when he returns to reign on earth, and it will continue without end into the eternal state.","support":"The promise of justice and peace established on David's throne describes a visible order on earth, which premillennialists think the present age does not fulfil. Luke 1:32-33 promises Jesus a reign 'over the house of Jacob', which they expect to be openly realised when he returns.","objection":"Amillennialists answer that the eternal character of the reign does not fit a limited millennium, and postmillennialists that it fits the present reign.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","grudem-systematic"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"The birth was fulfilled at the first advent, but the reign on David's throne is future: Christ will sit on David's literal throne in Jerusalem in the millennium and reign forever. Between the two lies the church age, which the prophet did not see. This is a classic case of prophetic “gap”.","support":"Luke 1:32-33 promises Jesus the throne of his father David and a reign over the house of Jacob, which dispensationalists take as national and literal. Jesus did not reign over Israel in that sense at his first coming.","objection":"Critics argue that Peter in Acts 2:30-36 says Christ was raised and exalted precisely to sit on David's throne.","sources":["walvoord-millennial-kingdom","pentecost-things-to-come"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"Christ's government was fully established in AD 70 when the old covenant age ended, and it now increases forever; there is no future consummation in which the kingdom changes.","support":"Full preterists see the present eternal reign without end as the kingdom fully come, which fits the endless increase of 9:7.","objection":"Critics argue that the full justice and peace described is not present in the world, and that the view leaves no future for the bodily resurrection confessed in the creeds.","sources":["king-spirit-of-prophecy","mathison-when-shall"]}],"sources":["motyer-prophecy-isaiah","boettner-millennium","walvoord-millennial-kingdom"]},{"id":"isaiah-65","title":"New Heavens and a New Earth","ref":"Isaiah 65:17–25","ranges":["Isaiah 65:17-25"],"category":"prophets","summary":"God announces that he creates new heavens and a new earth; Jerusalem will be a joy; weeping will not be heard. Yet the passage goes on to say that one who dies at a hundred will be considered young, and the sinner who reaches a hundred is accursed (65:20). People will build and plant and enjoy their work; the wolf and the lamb will feed together (65:25).","keyIssue":"The passage calls itself “new heavens and a new earth” but still includes death, sinners and a long lifespan. Is this the eternal state described in the language of Isaiah's time (with long life as a picture of no death), a millennial kingdom with death still present, or the new covenant era (the gospel age), which partial preterists and postmillennialists call the “new creation” already begun?","timeText":false,"topics":["millennium","kingdom","optimism","final-judgment"],"readings":[{"position":"postmil","reading":"This is the new creation already begun in Christ (2 Corinthians 5:17), described in the gospel age as it reaches maturity. Because death and sin still exist, it cannot be the eternal state. It describes the long-term flourishing of Christ's kingdom in history, with prosperity, long life, peace and justice increasing as the gospel transforms society.","support":"Death and sinners are present (65:20), which excludes the eternal state; but the passage also calls itself new creation, which fits the new covenant era inaugurated by Christ.","objection":"Amillennialists answer that Revelation 21:1-4, which takes up this prophecy, applies it to the eternal state with no death. Premillennialists answer that the passage describes conditions after a decisive divine intervention, not a gradual process.","sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","boettner-millennium"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"This is the eternal state, but Isaiah describes it in the terms of his own time: a lifespan of a hundred years would be extraordinary to the original hearers, a way of saying death has lost its power. Revelation 21:1-4 confirms that the reference is to the consummated new creation, and the fulfilment begins with Christ's first advent.","support":"Isaiah himself calls the scene new heavens and a new earth (65:17; 66:22), and Revelation 21 takes it up for the eternal state. Prophetic language naturally pictures the future in present-day images.","objection":"Premillennialists and postmillennialists argue that a sinner dying at a hundred cannot describe a world without sin and death; reading it as symbolic seems to evade the text.","sources":["riddlebarger-isaiah-65","hoekema-bible-future","motyer-prophecy-isaiah"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"The passage describes the millennial kingdom, in which life is long but death and sin are not yet removed, a stage before the eternal state that only premillennialism has a place for. Grudem lists Isaiah 65:20 among the main Old Testament supports for a future millennium.","support":"The conditions (death, but at an advanced age; a curse on sinners; animals at peace) fit neither the present age nor the eternal state, which suggests an intermediate earthly kingdom.","objection":"Critics answer that the passage itself calls the period “new heavens and new earth”, which Revelation 21 uses for the eternal state, so splitting the passage between millennium and eternity is hard to justify.","sources":["grudem-systematic","blomberg-chung-historic-premil"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"Many dispensationalists (Walvoord, Pentecost) take 65:17-19 as looking to the eternal state and 65:20-25 as describing the millennial kingdom, centred on Jerusalem, with long life and restored nature. Others read the whole passage as millennial, with the eternal state as its final form.","support":"The presence of death, building, planting and childbirth requires an earthly, pre-eternal kingdom in which Israel is restored in its land.","objection":"Critics argue that a shift from the eternal state to the millennium mid-passage has no textual marker, and that the passage is consistent if read either as a single picture of the consummation or as a picture of the present age.","sources":["walvoord-millennial-kingdom","pentecost-things-to-come"]},{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"The new heavens and new earth are the new covenant order that replaced the old covenant world (Hebrews 8:13). It was established in Christ and confirmed when the old order ended in AD 70. It then develops in history, which is why death and sin can still appear in it. A final consummation follows at Christ's return.","support":"Old Testament “heavens and earth” language can describe a covenant order (Isaiah 51:15-16; Deuteronomy 32:1), and the new covenant is new creation (2 Corinthians 5:17; Galatians 6:15).","objection":"Critics argue that 2 Peter 3:10-13 and Revelation 21 describe a cosmic renewal and the removal of death, which has not yet occurred, so the covenantal reading at most captures part of the meaning.","sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","demar-last-days-madness"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"The new heavens and new earth of Isaiah 65 and Revelation 21 are the new covenant world, fully arrived in AD 70. There is no future cosmic renewal; the passage describes the present spiritual reality of the church.","support":"Full preterists argue that the passage itself shows that the new heavens and earth contain death and sin, which makes it the present age rather than a future perfect world.","objection":"Critics answer that Revelation 21:4 explicitly removes death, and that Romans 8:18-23 expects creation itself to be freed from corruption. They note that denying a future renewal departs from historic orthodoxy.","sources":["king-spirit-of-prophecy","mathison-when-shall"]}],"sources":["riddlebarger-isaiah-65","motyer-prophecy-isaiah","walvoord-millennial-kingdom","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"]},{"id":"ezekiel-37-one-nation-one-shepherd","title":"One Nation, One Shepherd, One Sanctuary","ref":"Ezekiel 37:21–28","ranges":["Ezekiel 37:21-28"],"category":"prophets","summary":"After the vision of the dry bones (37:1–14) and the sign of the two sticks (37:15–20), God promises to gather Israel from the nations into its own land, make Judah and Ephraim one nation under one king, 'my servant David', cleanse them from idolatry, make an everlasting covenant of peace, and set his sanctuary among them forever, so that the nations will know the LORD sanctifies Israel.","keyIssue":"Is this a promise of a future national, territorial restoration of ethnic Israel under the Messiah, or is it fulfilled in Christ (the Davidic shepherd) and his one new-covenant people, as the New Testament's reuse of 37:27 in 2 Corinthians 6:16 and Revelation 21:3 suggests?","timeText":false,"topics":["israel","kingdom","millennium"],"readings":[{"position":"disppremil","reading":"A literal promise to the physical descendants of Jacob: regathering to the land God gave Jacob (37:25), national reunification, and the reign of the Messiah, David's greater Son, from Jerusalem during the millennium. Many dispensationalists see the modern return to the land (1948 onward) as a preparatory, still-unbelieving stage (compare the bones assembling before breath enters, 37:7–10), with spiritual regeneration to follow at Christ's return.","support":"The text is concrete and geographical ('the land I gave to my servant Jacob, where your fathers lived'), and the promise is called unconditional and everlasting (37:25–26). A consistent grammatical-historical reading, it is argued, should not transfer promises made to Israel to a different referent.","objection":"Critics reply that the New Testament itself applies the covenant formula of 37:27 to the mixed Jewish-Gentile church (2 Corinthians 6:16) and the 'one shepherd' theme to Jesus gathering 'other sheep' (John 10:16), indicating that the apostles read the passage as fulfilled in Christ. Dispensationalists answer that an application to the church does not exhaust the original promise to Israel.","sources":["pentecost-things-to-come","feinberg-prophecy-ezekiel","vlach-he-will-reign-forever"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"The promise is fulfilled in Jesus, the true David and good shepherd, who gathers one flock from Israel and the nations; the 'sanctuary among them' is God's dwelling in Christ and the church as temple, consummated in the new creation where 'the dwelling of God is with man' (Revelation 21:3). Land promises expand to the renewed earth (Romans 4:13; Matthew 5:5).","support":"The New Testament reuses this very passage for the church (2 Corinthians 6:16) and for the final state (Revelation 21:3); Jesus identifies himself as the one shepherd (John 10:11–16); Ephesians 2:14–22 describes the uniting of divided peoples into one temple.","objection":"Dispensationalists object that this is replacement theology that empties the 'David', 'land' and 'Judah and Ephraim' particulars of meaning. Amillennialists respond that fulfilment in Christ is not replacement but escalation: believing Israel is the root of the one people, and the promises are kept 'in a more glorious way'.","sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil","beale-temple-church-mission"]},{"position":"postmil","reading":"Agrees that the passage is fulfilled in Christ and his church, but many postmillennialists (following the Puritan hope) add that Romans 11 predicts a large-scale conversion of ethnic Jews within history, which will be a striking instance of the 'one flock' and help usher in a period of gospel blessing among the nations.","support":"Romans 11:12, 15, 25–26 links Israel's 'fullness' to 'life from the dead' for the world; the Puritan and Princeton tradition read Ezekiel's restoration through that lens.","objection":"Amillennialists counter that 'all Israel' in Romans 11:26 may be the elect remnant across the age, not a future mass conversion; dispensationalists object that conversion without land restoration still spiritualizes 37:25.","sources":["murray-puritan-hope","boettner-millennium","merkle-romans-11"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"Historic premillennialists are divided. Ladd held that the New Testament reinterprets Old Testament Israel promises in the church, while others in the tradition expect a future turning of ethnic Israel and see a partial earthly fulfilment in Christ's millennial reign before the eternal state brings the 'everlasting' aspects to completion.","support":"The two-stage hope (millennium, then new creation) gives room for both an earthly reign of the Davidic king (Revelation 20) and an everlasting sanctuary (Revelation 21:3).","objection":"Dispensationalists think Ladd conceded too much; amillennialists think inserting a millennium adds a stage the passage does not require.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","blomberg-chung-historic-premil"]},{"position":"progressive-covenantalism","reading":"Jesus is the true Israel and Davidic king; the promise is fulfilled typologically in him and in all united to him, Jew and Gentile alike. The land functions as a type of the whole new creation, so the 'everlasting' land promise is kept in the renewed cosmos rather than in a territorial restoration in the present age.","support":"Reads Ezekiel 37 along the canonical storyline of the covenants, in which Christ fulfils Israel's vocation and the new covenant forms one people (Ephesians 2:11–22; Hebrews 8).","objection":"Dispensational critics say typology is used to override the plain sense of the text; covenant theologians say the view underplays continuity with the believing Israel of old.","sources":["gentry-wellum-kingdom-covenant","vlach-has-church-replaced-israel"]}],"sources":["pentecost-things-to-come","hoekema-bible-future","gentry-wellum-kingdom-covenant","murray-puritan-hope"]},{"id":"ezekiel-40-48-temple-vision","title":"Ezekiel's Temple Vision and the River of Life","ref":"Ezekiel 40–48","ranges":["Ezekiel 40:1-4","Ezekiel 47:1-12"],"category":"prophets","summary":"In 573 BC Ezekiel is taken in visions to a very high mountain and shown, by a man with a measuring line, a vast temple complex (chs. 40–43), the return of God's glory, regulations for priests, a prince, offerings and festivals (chs. 44–46), a life-giving river flowing from the temple that grows from ankle-deep to an uncrossable torrent and heals the Dead Sea (47:1–12), and a new allotment of the land around a city named 'The LORD Is There' (48:35).","keyIssue":"Is this a blueprint for a literal future temple with sacrifices (a millennial temple), or a symbolic vision of restored worship fulfilled in Christ, the church and the new creation? The renewed sin and atonement offerings (43:19–27; 45:15–17) are the crux, in light of Hebrews 9–10.","timeText":false,"topics":["millennium","israel","kingdom"],"readings":[{"position":"disppremil","reading":"A real temple to be built in Jerusalem for the millennial kingdom, from which Christ reigns. Older dispensationalists took the sacrifices as memorials of the cross, as the Lord's Supper is now; more recent writers (e.g. Hullinger) argue they provide ceremonial purification so that the holy presence of God can dwell among mortal, sinful people, without adding to Christ's atoning work.","support":"The vision supplies exact measurements, named gates, priestly divisions and tribal boundaries; such detail, it is argued, makes little sense as mere symbol. Isaiah 56:6–7, 60:7 and Zechariah 14:16–21 also envisage worship and offerings among the nations in the age to come.","objection":"Critics note that 45:15–17 says the offerings 'make atonement', which seems to clash with Hebrews 10:18 ('no longer any offering for sin'). Proponents answer that the Levitical sacrifices never removed sin either (Hebrews 10:4) but dealt with ritual purity, and that the same will be true of millennial sacrifices.","sources":["pentecost-things-to-come","hullinger-ezekiel-sacrifices","feinberg-prophecy-ezekiel"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"An apocalyptic-symbolic vision of the restored presence of God, fulfilled in Christ, who is the true temple (John 2:19–21), in the church as God's temple (1 Corinthians 3:16; Ephesians 2:21), and finally in the new Jerusalem, which has no temple because God and the Lamb are its temple (Revelation 21:22). The river of 47:1–12 reappears in Revelation 22:1–2 and in Jesus's promise of living water (John 7:37–39).","support":"Revelation 21–22 deliberately reworks Ezekiel 40–48 (measuring, the high mountain, twelve gates, the river and trees whose leaves heal); the symmetrical dimensions and perfect square suggest an idealized vision; Hebrews forbids a return to the shadows.","objection":"Dispensationalists respond that symbolic readings cannot explain why so much non-symbolic detail is given, and that John's use of Ezekiel does not mean Ezekiel had nothing literal in view.","sources":["beale-temple-church-mission","monergism-amil-ezekiel-40-48","hoekema-bible-future"]},{"position":"postmil","reading":"Shares the symbolic-fulfilment reading, but stresses the river of chapter 47 as a picture of the gospel's progressive growth in history: it deepens stage by stage until it fills the land and heals the Dead Sea, so the kingdom flowing from Christ's temple will gradually bring life to the nations before the end.","support":"The ever-deepening river matches the growth parables of Matthew 13:31–33 and the stone becoming a mountain (Daniel 2:35); the healing of the nations (Revelation 22:2) is taken as a process now under way.","objection":"Amillennialists caution that Revelation 22 places the river in the consummated new creation, not in pre-consummation history; dispensationalists hold that the river is geographically real (Zechariah 14:8).","sources":["chilton-paradise-restored","mathison-postmillennialism"]},{"position":"idealism","reading":"An idealized theological portrait of perfected worship that was never meant to be built: Ezekiel is shown what God's dwelling among a holy people must be, with access, holiness and life spreading outward. Some add that it held out a conditional pattern for the exiles (43:10–11), which they failed to implement, and that its permanent truth is realized in Christ and the new creation.","support":"Ezekiel receives no command to build (contrast Exodus 25:9); the dimensions are schematic; the land allotment ignores real geography; Block reads chs. 40–48 as a theological 'constitution' for restored Israel rather than a construction plan.","objection":"Literal interpreters reply that 'never meant to be built' makes much of the detail pointless; others note that the conditional view raises the question why the returnees did not adopt it.","sources":["block-ezekiel-25-48","gregg-ezekiel-temple-equip"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"Historic premillennialists vary: many follow a typological reading fulfilled in Christ and the new creation, while some allow that elements may have a literal expression in Christ's millennial reign, though without the restoration of atoning sacrifices.","support":"The historic-premillennial tradition does not rest its case on Old Testament temple prophecies but on Revelation 20, so it can read Ezekiel 40–48 with the typological tools of New Testament usage.","objection":"Dispensationalists see this as inconsistent: a literal millennium in Revelation 20 but a symbolic temple in Ezekiel. Amillennialists say a symbolic Ezekiel removes a main support for an earthly interregnum.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","gregg-ezekiel-temple-equip"]}],"sources":["gregg-ezekiel-temple-equip","beale-temple-church-mission","pentecost-things-to-come","block-ezekiel-25-48"]},{"id":"zechariah-12-10-pierced-one","title":"They Will Look on Me, the One They Have Pierced","ref":"Zechariah 12:10","ranges":["Zechariah 12:10"],"category":"prophets","summary":"In a section where the LORD defends Jerusalem against the nations (12:1–9), he promises to pour out on the house of David and Jerusalem a 'spirit of grace and supplication', so that they look on the one they pierced and mourn as for an only son, a mourning compared to that at Hadad-rimmon (12:11), followed by a fountain opened for cleansing from sin (13:1).","keyIssue":"When and how does this mourning happen? John 19:37 applies the verse to the crucifixion and Revelation 1:7 to a coming with the clouds. Is it fulfilled at the cross and Pentecost, at AD 70, or in a future national repentance of Israel at the Second Coming?","timeText":false,"topics":["israel","second-coming","olivet-generation"],"readings":[{"position":"disppremil","reading":"A future national repentance of Israel at the close of the Tribulation: as the nations besiege Jerusalem (12:2–9; 14:1–3), the surviving Jewish people recognize Jesus as the Messiah they rejected, mourn, and are cleansed (13:1), and Christ returns to deliver them. Some teachers make this national confession the trigger for the Second Coming (compare Hosea 5:15; Matthew 23:39).","support":"The setting is military and national (Jerusalem, the house of David, clan by clan, 12:12–14); it parallels Romans 11:26 ('all Israel will be saved') and Matthew 24:30, where the 'tribes' mourn when they see the Son of Man.","objection":"Critics point out that John 19:37 already treats the piercing as fulfilled at Calvary and that Acts 2:37–41 records Jerusalem's 'cut to the heart' repentance at Pentecost. Dispensationalists reply that the piercing was fulfilled at the cross while the national looking and mourning remain future.","sources":["fruchtenbaum-footsteps-messiah","walvoord-millennial-kingdom","ice-preterism-zechariah"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"The piercing is fulfilled at the crucifixion (John 19:37); the outpoured Spirit of grace and the repentance of Jerusalem's people begin at Pentecost (Acts 2:17, 37–38), and the fountain for cleansing is Christ's atoning work. Revelation 1:7 widens the circle to 'all the tribes of the earth', who will mourn at Christ's final, visible return.","support":"The New Testament's two citations bracket the age: the cross (John 19:37) and the consummation (Revelation 1:7). Peter's Pentecost sermon matches the elements of 12:10 (outpoured Spirit, guilt for the crucified one, mourning, cleansing in baptism).","objection":"Dispensationalists object that Pentecost involved thousands, not the house of David 'clan by clan'; postmillennialists add that Romans 11 still anticipates a broader turning of Israel.","sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil"]},{"position":"postmil","reading":"Fulfilled initially at the cross and Pentecost, and to be fulfilled more fully when the Spirit brings a widespread conversion of the Jewish people within history, as the Puritans expected from Romans 11; this conversion will be a great blessing to the world.","support":"Romans 11:12, 15 promises greater riches for the world through Israel's 'fullness'; the Spirit-wrought repentance of 12:10 fits a revival, not a coercive event at the Parousia.","objection":"Amillennialists question whether Romans 11 teaches a future mass conversion; dispensationalists argue the passage's battle setting points to the end of history, not a gradual revival.","sources":["murray-puritan-hope","boettner-millennium"]},{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"Revelation 1:7, which combines Daniel 7:13 with Zechariah 12:10, describes Christ's 'coming in clouds' of judgment on Jerusalem in AD 70: 'those who pierced him' are the first-century generation, and the 'tribes of the land' (Greek gē can mean land) are the clans of Judea mourning their judgment.","support":"Revelation's time markers ('soon', 1:1; 'near', 1:3) and Jesus's warning to the high priest that he would 'see' the Son of Man coming (Matthew 26:64) fit a first-century audience; Zechariah 12:12–14 itself speaks of Israelite clans.","objection":"Critics note that in Zechariah 12 the mourning is repentance granted by grace and Jerusalem is defended, not destroyed, so AD 70 is an odd fulfilment; Thomas Ice presses this point. Preterists respond that Revelation 1:7 reapplies Zechariah's imagery, and that mourning can be lament under judgment as well as repentance.","sources":["gentry-before-jerusalem-fell","chilton-days-vengeance","ice-preterism-zechariah"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"Both the looking and the coming of Revelation 1:7 were completed in AD 70, when Christ came in the Father's glory and judged the generation that pierced him; there is no further visible coming to await.","support":"Takes the time texts of Revelation and Matthew 26:64 as strictly limiting the fulfilment to the first-century generation, and notes that 'those who pierced him' (Revelation 1:7) names a specific first-century group who would see the coming.","objection":"This reading denies a future visible return, placing it outside the historic creeds (Nicene Creed, 'he will come again in glory'); critics also note Revelation 1:7 says 'every eye' will see him. Proponents answer that 'every eye' is idiomatic for those involved, as in Matthew 26:64.","sources":["russell-parousia","king-spirit-of-prophecy","mathison-when-shall"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"Agrees that the piercing was fulfilled at Calvary but expects a future spiritual turning of Israel at or just before Christ's return, linked to Romans 11:26 and followed by Christ's earthly reign.","support":"Holds together John 19:37, Revelation 1:7 and Romans 11 without requiring a pretribulational framework.","objection":"Amillennialists question the future mass-conversion premise; dispensationalists see the historic-premillennial treatment of Israel as underdeveloped.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","blomberg-chung-historic-premil"]}],"sources":["fruchtenbaum-footsteps-messiah","hoekema-bible-future","gentry-before-jerusalem-fell","ice-preterism-zechariah"]},{"id":"zechariah-14-day-of-the-lord","title":"The Day of the LORD on the Mount of Olives","ref":"Zechariah 14:1–11","ranges":["Zechariah 14:1-11"],"category":"prophets","summary":"All nations gather to battle against Jerusalem; the city is taken and half its people exiled (14:1–2). Then the LORD goes out to fight; his feet stand on the Mount of Olives, which splits east and west (14:3–5). A unique day follows, living waters flow from Jerusalem east and west, the LORD becomes king over all the earth, and Jerusalem dwells securely (14:6–11). Later verses (14:16–21) have survivors of the nations going up yearly to keep the Feast of Booths.","keyIssue":"Is this a literal end-time battle and topographical change at Christ's return before an earthly millennium, a symbolic portrait of God's victory fulfilled in Christ and the church, or a picture partly fulfilled in the Roman siege of AD 70 and the gospel's spread from Jerusalem?","timeText":false,"topics":["millennium","second-coming","israel","kingdom"],"readings":[{"position":"disppremil","reading":"A future siege of Jerusalem at the end of the Tribulation (Armageddon), ended by Christ's bodily return to the Mount of Olives (compare Acts 1:11–12), a real earthquake reshaping the land, and the start of his millennial reign. Because the nations who refuse to keep the Feast of Booths are punished (14:16–19), sin and death still exist: this cannot be the eternal state, so it must be the millennium.","support":"The geographic precision (Mount of Olives 'east of Jerusalem', specific gates and towers, 14:10), the link with Acts 1:11–12, and the presence of disobedience after the Lord becomes king all support an earthly interregnum.","objection":"Non-dispensationalists answer that prophetic idiom (compare Micah 1:3–4) often uses mountains splitting for theophany, and that the reinstated Feast of Booths with temple vessels and cooking pots (14:20–21) is the language of Israel's worship used for a reality the New Testament fulfils in Christ.","sources":["walvoord-millennial-kingdom","pentecost-things-to-come","ice-preterism-zechariah"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"An apocalyptic picture of the decisive victory of God over the nations and the universal reign of the LORD, fulfilled in Christ's first coming, the outpouring of the Spirit (the 'living waters' of 14:8, compare John 7:37–39 and Ezekiel 47), the ingathering of the nations into the worship of God, and the final consummation where night ceases (14:7; Revelation 22:5).","support":"Revelation picks up Zechariah 14's motifs (no night, light from God, living water, 'no longer any curse', 14:11; Revelation 22:3) for the new creation; the 'holy to the LORD' inscription spread to ordinary pots (14:20–21) signals the end of the sacred-common distinction realized in Christ.","objection":"Dispensationalists object that the 'symbolic' reading is selective, conceding the literal Mount of Olives in Acts 1 while denying it here; they also ask how the eternal state can contain nations that refuse to worship (14:17).","sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil","beale-temple-church-mission"]},{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"Verses 1–2 describe Rome's siege and capture of Jerusalem in AD 70, a 'day of the LORD' in which God used the nations to judge the city; the LORD's going out and the waters flowing from Jerusalem are the gospel's advance from Jerusalem to the nations (Acts 1:8), and the Lord's universal kingship is Christ's present reign.","support":"Jesus's Olivet prophecy (spoken on the Mount of Olives) echoes the capture, the plundering and flight of 14:2–5 (Luke 21:20–24), and Acts 1–2 begins the outflow from Jerusalem.","objection":"Ice argues that Zechariah 14 shows the LORD rescuing Jerusalem and fighting against its attackers, the reverse of AD 70; preterists answer that 14:1–2 is judgment on the city and 14:3ff. the vindication of the faithful remnant and church, who fled and survived (Luke 21:21).","sources":["chilton-days-vengeance","demar-last-days-madness","ice-preterism-zechariah"]},{"position":"postmil","reading":"Emphasizes 14:9 ('the LORD will be king over all the earth') and 14:16–21 as a progressive historical triumph: through the gospel, the nations increasingly come to worship the true God, and the whole of life becomes 'holy to the LORD' before Christ's final return.","support":"Coheres with Psalm 22:27–28, Isaiah 2:2–4 and the growth parables; the 'holiness' inscribed on bells and cooking pots pictures the sanctification of culture.","objection":"Amillennialists respond that the passage's full realization is the consummated new creation, not pre-Parousia history; dispensationalists object that Christ's personal presence on the Mount of Olives (14:4) precedes the universal reign.","sources":["chilton-paradise-restored","mathison-postmillennialism"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"A future event at Christ's return, which inaugurates his millennial reign over the nations; the details may be literal or a mixture of literal and symbolic, but the sequence of coming, victory and a reign with remaining disobedience fits Revelation 19–20.","support":"Like dispensationalists, historic premillennialists find 14:16–19 hard to fit into either the present age or the eternal state.","objection":"Amillennialists say the passage portrays one final victory, not two stages; partial preterists see 14:1–2 as AD 70.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","grudem-systematic"]}],"sources":["walvoord-millennial-kingdom","ice-preterism-zechariah","chilton-days-vengeance","hoekema-bible-future"]},{"id":"matthew-13-kingdom-parables","title":"Wheat and Tares, Mustard Seed and Leaven","ref":"Matthew 13:24–43","ranges":["Matthew 13:24-43"],"category":"gospels","summary":"Jesus gives three kingdom parables: an enemy sows weeds (zizania, probably darnel) among wheat, and the owner lets both grow until the harvest, when the weeds are burned and the wheat gathered (13:24–30, explained in 13:36–43 as the judgment at 'the end of the age', synteleia tou aiōnos); a mustard seed, smallest of seeds, becomes a tree in which birds nest (13:31–32); and a little leaven works through three measures of flour until all is leavened (13:33).","keyIssue":"What do the parables teach about the kingdom between the two comings: steady growth to world-wide influence, a mixed field with good and evil coexisting until the end, or a 'mystery form' of the kingdom that includes corruption? And is 'the end of the age' the final judgment or the end of the Jewish age in AD 70?","timeText":false,"topics":["kingdom","optimism","final-judgment","church-history"],"readings":[{"position":"postmil","reading":"The mustard seed and leaven promise that Christ's kingdom, though tiny in its beginnings, will grow extensively (filling the world, Daniel 2:35) and intensively (permeating every sphere of life) through the gospel before the harvest at Christ's return. The wheat and tares show the kingdom advancing in a world where evil persists until the end, but not that evil keeps pace with it.","support":"Leaven works 'until all was leavened' (13:33), a picture of total permeation; birds nesting in branches echoes Daniel 4:12 and Ezekiel 17:23, where nations find shelter under a great kingdom.","objection":"Amillennialists answer that the parables stress the contrast of small beginning and large ending, not a timetable or a dominant majority in history; dispensationalists say leaven is consistently a symbol of evil (Matthew 16:6; 1 Corinthians 5:6–8). Postmillennialists reply that context, not a fixed code, decides a symbol's meaning, and Jesus says the kingdom itself is like leaven.","sources":["kik-eschatology-of-victory","boettner-millennium","mathison-postmillennialism"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"The kingdom is truly present and growing through the church age, but it grows in a world where the enemy's sowing continues side by side until the harvest; the age ends with a single judgment separating righteous and wicked. No pre-consummation golden age is promised: the kingdom's triumph is real but hidden, and its full manifestation awaits Christ's return.","support":"Wheat and weeds grow together 'until the harvest' (13:30), and the harvest is 'the end of the age' with one judgment of both groups (13:40–43), leaving no room for a millennium in between or a near-total eradication of evil before the end.","objection":"Postmillennialists say that 'coexistence' does not exclude 'proportion': the wheat can vastly outnumber the weeds. Premillennialists say a single harvest scene does not rule out an intervening kingdom phase revealed elsewhere (Revelation 20).","sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil","ridderbos-coming-kingdom"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"These parables describe the 'mystery form' of the kingdom during the interadvent age, after Israel's rejection of the King and before the millennial kingdom. Classic dispensationalists (e.g. Scofield, Pentecost) read the abnormally large mustard plant with birds (compare 13:4, 19, where birds are the evil one) and the leaven as pictures of corruption entering Christendom, so the age ends in apostasy rather than global conversion.","support":"Jesus speaks of 'the mysteries of the kingdom' (13:11), suggesting something previously unrevealed; elsewhere in Matthew leaven means false teaching (16:6, 12), and the birds in this same chapter are agents of Satan.","objection":"Critics note that 13:33 says 'the kingdom of heaven is like leaven', making leaven a positive figure for the kingdom itself; and a mustard 'tree' with nesting birds echoes positive Old Testament imagery of nations finding shelter. Some progressive dispensationalists accept this and read the parables as growth parables.","sources":["pentecost-things-to-come","walvoord-millennial-kingdom","blaising-bock-progressive"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"Ladd's 'mystery of the kingdom': God's reign has entered history in a hidden, unexpected form, working secretly like seed and leaven without overpowering evil; it will be manifested in power at the Parousia, followed by the millennium and the age to come.","support":"Explains why the kingdom foretold in Daniel 2 came first as a small, resistible presence rather than a world-crushing stone, and keeps both present reality and future consummation.","objection":"Postmillennialists say Ladd underplays the growth and final world-filling scope of the images; amillennialists see no need for the millennium Ladd adds after the harvest.","sources":["ladd-gospel-kingdom","ladd-presence-future"]},{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"Partial preterists divide. Many (e.g. Gentry) read the harvest at 'the end of the age' as the final judgment; others (e.g. DeMar) note that synteleia tou aiōnos echoes the disciples' question in 24:3 and can denote the close of the old covenant age in AD 70, with the growth parables describing the kingdom's spread after that decisive judgment.","support":"Matthew uses the same phrase in 24:3, which many link to the temple's destruction; Hebrews 9:26 places Christ's appearing 'at the end of the ages'.","objection":"Critics reply that 13:41–43 involves angels removing all lawbreakers and the righteous shining in the Father's kingdom, which fits final judgment better than AD 70; this is why many partial preterists, and all creedal readers, keep it future.","sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","demar-last-days-madness"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"The harvest at the end of the age was the AD 70 judgment ending the Mosaic age, when the 'sons of the kingdom' who rejected Christ were cast out and the righteous shone in the established kingdom; the kingdom continues to grow after that point.","support":"Holds 13:39–40 and 24:3 to the same 'end of the age', which Jesus placed within his generation (24:34).","objection":"This view denies a future final judgment at Christ's return, placing it outside historic creedal orthodoxy (Westminster Confession 33); critics also note the universal scope of 'all causes of sin and all lawbreakers' (13:41). Proponents answer that the creeds must yield to Scripture's time statements.","sources":["king-spirit-of-prophecy","preston-eschatology-org","mathison-when-shall"]}],"sources":["kik-eschatology-of-victory","hoekema-bible-future","pentecost-things-to-come","ladd-gospel-kingdom"]},{"id":"matthew-28-great-commission","title":"The Great Commission","ref":"Matthew 28:18–20","ranges":["Matthew 28:18-20"],"category":"gospels","summary":"The risen Jesus declares that all authority in heaven and on earth has been given to him (echoing Daniel 7:14), commands the eleven to 'make disciples of all nations' (panta ta ethnē), baptizing and teaching them to observe all he commanded, and promises to be with them always, 'to the end of the age' (synteleia tou aiōnos).","keyIssue":"Does the commission imply its own success, the discipling of nations as nations so that the world is largely Christianized before Christ returns, or does it command a faithful worldwide witness that gathers a people from every nation while the world remains mixed or worsens?","timeText":false,"topics":["optimism","kingdom","church-history"],"readings":[{"position":"postmil","reading":"The commission rests on Christ's universal authority (Daniel 7:13–14 fulfilled at his resurrection and ascension) and therefore implies its own accomplishment: whole nations will be discipled, baptized and taught to obey Christ, with his presence guaranteeing the result. History will see the gospel's widespread triumph before 'the end of the age'.","support":"The command is grounded in authority already possessed, not in a hoped-for future; 'make disciples of all nations' is more than 'preach to'; Psalm 2:8 and 22:27, Isaiah 2:2–4 and Daniel 2:35 promise global success.","objection":"Amillennialists answer that a command does not predict its degree of success, and Jesus elsewhere forecasts persecution and lawlessness (Matthew 24:9–12). Postmillennialists reply that these warnings refer largely to the pre-70 generation, and that Christ's authority makes defeat of the commission unthinkable.","sources":["gentry-greatness-great-commission","bahnsen-victory-in-jesus","mathison-postmillennialism"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"The commission will certainly be fulfilled in the sense God intends: a people is gathered from every nation (Revelation 5:9; 7:9) and Christ is present with his church until the end of the age, his final return. It does not promise that a majority of the world will be converted; faithful witness under suffering continues alongside the growth of opposition.","support":"'All nations' in Matthew typically means peoples from every nation (24:14; 25:32); the age ends with both wheat and weeds present (13:30); the New Testament expects tribulation for the church throughout the age (John 16:33; Acts 14:22).","objection":"Postmillennialists say this reduces Christ's universal authority to a remnant-gathering task; amillennialists answer that the gathering of a countless multitude from every tribe is no small victory and that God's sovereignty does not require statistical majorities.","sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil","storms-kingdom-come"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"The mandate of the church age is world evangelization and the calling out of a people for Christ's name (Acts 15:14), not the Christianization of nations. The age ends in apostasy and the Tribulation; the nations are judged and the kingdom established by Christ himself at his return (Matthew 25:31–46).","support":"2 Timothy 3:1–5, 2 Thessalonians 2:3 and Luke 18:8 forecast decline; Christ, not the church, establishes the visible kingdom.","objection":"Postmillennialists object that this outlook reads the commission's grounding in Christ's present authority too lightly; amillennialists agree that the commission is evangelistic but deny a separate church-age parenthesis. Dispensationalists answer that Christ's authority is fully at work in gathering his church from all nations now, and will be displayed openly when he returns to reign.","sources":["walvoord-millennial-kingdom","ryrie-dispensationalism"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"The church proclaims the gospel to all nations as a witness (Matthew 24:14), a task that is itself a sign of the end and must be completed before Christ returns to establish his millennial reign; the kingdom's present power operates in mission, but its consummation awaits the Parousia.","support":"Ladd stressed that the church's mission is the means by which God's reign advances now, giving missionary urgency without promising a Christianized world.","objection":"Postmillennialists say Ladd's 'witness' framing understates the command to disciple nations; amillennialists accept most of the reading but not the millennium.","sources":["ladd-gospel-kingdom","ladd-blessed-hope"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"Many full preterists hold that the universal-preaching benchmark (Matthew 24:14) was met before AD 70 (Colossians 1:6, 23; Romans 10:18), and that 'the end of the age' was the close of the Mosaic age at that time. Most still regard evangelism as an ongoing duty in the endless new covenant age; a minority question whether the commission as such binds the church today.","support":"Paul's statements that the gospel had gone to 'all creation under heaven' (Colossians 1:23) are taken as signalling that the end of 24:14 arrived in that generation.","objection":"Critics argue that the promise 'I am with you always' and the command to disciple all nations hardly expired in AD 70, and that Paul's language is hyperbolic or representative; full preterism's denial of a future Second Coming also places it outside the historic creeds.","sources":["preston-eschatology-org","mathison-when-shall"]}],"sources":["gentry-greatness-great-commission","hoekema-bible-future","walvoord-millennial-kingdom"]},{"id":"matthew-10-23-cities-of-israel","title":"Before You Finish the Cities of Israel","ref":"Matthew 10:23","ranges":["Matthew 10:23"],"category":"gospels","summary":"Sending the Twelve on mission, Jesus warns of persecution and says that when they are persecuted in one town they should flee to the next, for they 'will not have gone through the towns of Israel before the Son of Man comes'.","keyIssue":"What 'coming' of the Son of Man fits a timeframe shorter than completing a mission to Israel's towns? Albert Schweitzer made the verse the cornerstone of his view that Jesus mistakenly expected the end during the disciples' first tour.","timeText":true,"topics":["olivet-generation","second-coming","israel"],"readings":[{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"The coming is Christ's coming in judgment on Jerusalem in AD 70: the disciples' mission to Israel would not be completed before the Son of Man came, as Daniel 7 foretold, to vindicate himself and end the old order. The persecution described (synagogue floggings, governors and kings, 10:17–18) fits the apostolic generation in Acts.","support":"The time limit is real and short; 10:17–22 reaches beyond the first tour to the whole period of apostolic witness; Matthew 16:28 and 24:34 place a 'coming' within the generation.","objection":"Critics ask why a 'coming' in Roman armies is called the Son of Man's coming; preterists answer that the Old Testament speaks of the LORD 'coming on the clouds' in historical judgments (Isaiah 19:1). Others argue the mission to Israel was hardly 'not completed' by AD 70 in any meaningful sense.","sources":["france-matthew-nicnt","gentry-before-jerusalem-fell","demar-last-days-madness"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"The verse refers to the one Parousia of Christ, which occurred in AD 70 within the lifetime of those addressed. Russell and later preterists cite it as evidence that the New Testament expected one coming only, in that generation.","support":"Matthew elsewhere uses 'the coming of the Son of Man' for the Parousia (24:27, 37, 39); the time limit is strict.","objection":"Denying a future bodily return places the view outside historic creedal orthodoxy; critics also note that the New Testament describes the Second Coming as visible and universal (Acts 1:11; 1 Thessalonians 4:16).","sources":["russell-parousia","king-spirit-of-prophecy","mathison-when-shall"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"Many Reformed interpreters see a 'coming' in the vindication events that follow the cross: Jesus's resurrection and enthronement (28:18), the gift of the Spirit, and the judgment of AD 70 as stages of the one coming of the Son of Man to receive the kingdom, which is consummated at his final return.","support":"Ridderbos's 'fulfilment in stages' explains why the Gospels attach both near and far time markers to the Son of Man's coming; Matthew 26:64 says 'from now on' the Son of Man will be seen.","objection":"Some say this multiplies 'comings' to escape a difficulty; others ask why the same phrase means different events in nearby texts.","sources":["ridderbos-coming-kingdom","carson-matthew-ebc","meyer-matthew-10-23"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"The coming is the Second Coming: the mission of Christ's followers to the Jewish people will continue, amid persecution, and will not be completed before the Son of Man returns. On this reading the verse is a promise of continuing mission to Israel, not a deadline.","support":"10:17–22 broadens beyond the first tour to the whole age; Matthew's other uses of 'the Son of Man comes' point to the Parousia; Carson argues the saying assures continued opportunity in Israel until the end.","objection":"The word 'finish' (teleō) sounds like a time limit on a task, not open-ended continuation; preterists see this as reading in a long delay.","sources":["carson-matthew-ebc","meyer-matthew-10-23","ladd-presence-future"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"Many dispensationalists read the mission to Israel as interrupted by Israel's rejection of the King and resumed during the future Tribulation by Jewish witnesses (Revelation 7:1–8), who will not finish going through Israel's towns before Christ returns. Others favour a near reference to Jesus rejoining the disciples after their mission.","support":"The kingdom offer was to Israel first (10:5–6); the persecution described in 10:17–22 resembles Tribulation conditions in Matthew 24:9–14.","objection":"Critics object that the instructions were given to the Twelve in their own day, and that a gap of millennia in the middle of a single paragraph is hard to justify.","sources":["pentecost-things-to-come","walvoord-millennial-kingdom","meyer-matthew-10-23"]}],"sources":["meyer-matthew-10-23","france-matthew-nicnt","carson-matthew-ebc"]},{"id":"matthew-16-27-28-some-standing-here","title":"Some Standing Here Will Not Taste Death","ref":"Matthew 16:27–28","ranges":["Matthew 16:27-28"],"category":"gospels","summary":"After calling disciples to take up the cross, Jesus says the Son of Man will come in his Father's glory with his angels and repay each person according to what he has done (16:27), and then that some standing there will not taste death before they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom (16:28). Mark 9:1 and Luke 9:27 speak of seeing 'the kingdom of God' (Mark: 'come with power'); all three Gospels place the Transfiguration immediately after.","keyIssue":"What did 'some' of the bystanders see before dying, and is verse 27 the same coming as verse 28?","timeText":true,"topics":["second-coming","olivet-generation","kingdom","final-judgment"],"readings":[{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"Verse 28 refers to Christ's coming in judgment and kingdom vindication in AD 70, which 'some', not all, of the disciples lived to see. Many partial preterists (e.g. Gentry) distinguish verse 27, the final judgment of every person, from verse 28; others (e.g. DeMar) read both verses of AD 70, with 27 describing covenantal judgment on that generation.","support":"'Some will not taste death' implies a span long enough that most bystanders would die, which suits roughly forty years better than six days; the wording matches 24:30–34 and 26:64.","objection":"Critics note that the Synoptic placement before the Transfiguration, and Peter's own appeal to the Transfiguration as eyewitness of Christ's 'power and coming' (2 Peter 1:16–18), point to a nearer fulfilment.","sources":["sproul-last-days","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","demar-last-days-madness"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"Verses 27 and 28 describe one event: the Son of Man came in glory with his angels, rendered judgment to each, and established his kingdom in AD 70, within the lifetime of some hearers. Because the repayment of 'each person' was then, the final judgment is past.","support":"The 'for' (gar) linking the verses and the unbroken flow from glory, angels and judgment to 'some standing here' are taken to forbid splitting them.","objection":"Treating the judgment of 'each person' as past places the view outside historic creedal orthodoxy; critics also argue that 16:27 echoes the universal judgment of Psalm 62:12 and Romans 2:6. Proponents answer that the creeds cannot outrank Jesus's time limit.","sources":["russell-parousia","preston-eschatology-org","mathison-when-shall"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"The Transfiguration, six days later, is a preview and pledge of the Son of Man's kingdom glory, seen by Peter, James and John; it begins a series (resurrection, ascension, Pentecost, and arguably AD 70) by which Jesus's kingdom 'comes with power', all anticipating the final coming of 16:27.","support":"All three Synoptics attach the saying directly to the Transfiguration; Mark's version ('the kingdom of God come with power') fits Pentecost and the resurrection; 2 Peter 1:16–18 calls the Transfiguration a witness of Christ's 'power and coming'.","objection":"Preterists object that 'some will not taste death' is a strange way to describe an event six days away when none had died; amillennialists answer that the expression stresses privilege and certainty rather than delay.","sources":["mathison-some-standing-here","carson-matthew-ebc","ridderbos-coming-kingdom"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"The Transfiguration is a preview of Christ's future millennial kingdom glory: Peter, James and John saw in miniature the King coming in his kingdom, while 16:27 refers to the future Second Coming and judgment.","support":"2 Peter 1:16–18 explicitly connects the Transfiguration to Christ's 'coming'; the immediate narrative sequence; Moses and Elijah suggest the kingdom with glorified and living saints.","objection":"Critics note the plural 'some' and 'not taste death' suggest a longer interval, and partial preterists argue the Transfiguration was a vision of glory, not a 'coming in his kingdom'.","sources":["pentecost-things-to-come","walvoord-millennial-kingdom"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"The kingdom 'come with power' (Mark 9:1) refers to manifestations of God's reign within the disciples' lifetime, beginning with the Transfiguration and resurrection and especially Pentecost; the Parousia of 16:27 remains future and inaugurates the millennium.","support":"Ladd's already/not-yet: the kingdom is God's dynamic reign, which can 'come with power' before its consummation.","objection":"Preterists say this fails to explain the 'Son of Man coming' wording in Matthew; others see it as merging distinct events.","sources":["ladd-presence-future","ladd-gospel-kingdom"]}],"sources":["mathison-some-standing-here","sproul-last-days","carson-matthew-ebc"]},{"id":"john-5-28-29-hour-of-resurrection","title":"An Hour Is Coming When All in the Tombs Will Hear","ref":"John 5:28–29","ranges":["John 5:28-29"],"category":"gospels","summary":"Jesus says the hour is coming and now is when the spiritually dead hear the Son's voice and live (5:25), then adds that an hour is coming when all who are in the tombs will hear his voice and come out, those who have done good to the resurrection of life and those who have done evil to the resurrection of judgment (5:28–29), echoing Daniel 12:2.","keyIssue":"Does 'an hour' teach a single general resurrection of righteous and wicked together, ruling out a thousand-year gap between two resurrections (Revelation 20:4–6)? And is the resurrection of 5:28–29 bodily and future, or fulfilled spiritually or corporately?","timeText":false,"topics":["first-resurrection","final-judgment","millennium","second-coming"],"readings":[{"position":"amil","reading":"One general resurrection of all people, righteous and wicked, at Christ's return, followed by one final judgment. The 'first resurrection' of Revelation 20:5 is the believer's present spiritual life with Christ or the soul's entrance into heaven, as 5:25 describes a present spiritual resurrection followed in 5:28–29 by a future bodily one.","support":"Both groups rise in the same 'hour' at the same voice; Daniel 12:2 and Acts 24:15 likewise speak of one resurrection 'of both the just and the unjust'; John 6:39–54 places believers' resurrection on 'the last day'.","objection":"Premillennialists reply that John's own 'hour' in 5:25 has already lasted two thousand years, so an 'hour' can span a millennium. Amillennialists answer that 5:25 is a spiritual hour by its nature, whereas 5:28–29 concerns a single bodily event at the last day.","sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil","augustine-city-of-god"]},{"position":"postmil","reading":"Agrees with the amillennial reading: one bodily general resurrection and judgment at Christ's single return after the millennial period of gospel prosperity; 5:25 describes the spiritual resurrection by which the kingdom grows now.","support":"The two-stage pattern (spiritual resurrection now, bodily resurrection at the end) is how Augustine and the Reformed confessions understood Revelation 20 and John 5.","objection":"Premillennialists raise the same 'hour' objection; full preterists say the bodily-future reading ignores the near-coming context of John's eschatology.","sources":["warfield-millennium-apocalypse","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","westminster-confession"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"Jesus speaks of the resurrection of both groups without specifying whether they occur together; as 'the hour' of 5:25 spans the whole church age, the 'hour' of 5:28 can contain both the resurrection of the righteous at Christ's return and the resurrection of the rest after the thousand years (Revelation 20:5).","support":"Revelation 20:4–6 explicitly separates a first resurrection of the blessed from 'the rest of the dead'; 1 Corinthians 15:23–24 lists an order ('each in his own order').","objection":"Amillennialists respond that John's Gospel consistently places the righteous's resurrection 'on the last day' (6:39–54), leaving no later day for the wicked to be raised.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","grudem-systematic"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"There are distinct resurrections in sequence: of the church at the rapture, of Old Testament and Tribulation saints at Christ's return, and of the wicked after the millennium (Revelation 20:5, 11–15). John 5:28–29 describes the two kinds of resurrection, not their timing.","support":"Revelation 20 gives a thousand-year gap; 'the resurrection of life' and 'the resurrection of judgment' are qualitatively distinct, like the 'better resurrection' of Hebrews 11:35.","objection":"Critics say this multiplies resurrections beyond the text and conflicts with the single 'hour' and single 'last day' of John's Gospel.","sources":["walvoord-millennial-kingdom","pentecost-things-to-come"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"The resurrection of 5:28–29 occurred in AD 70, when the dead were raised from Hades (the realm of the dead) into God's presence and the judgment took place; it was not the reanimation of physical corpses. Some, following Max King, see a corporate resurrection of the covenant people from the 'death' of the old covenant.","support":"Ties 5:28–29 to Daniel 12:2, whose fulfilment Daniel 12:7 places at the shattering of the holy people's power, which proponents identify with AD 70.","objection":"Denying a future bodily resurrection places the view outside historic creedal orthodoxy (Nicene and Apostles' Creeds); Wright and others argue that 'resurrection' in Jewish and Christian usage meant bodily life after death, and 'all in the tombs' denotes bodies. Proponents answer that Hades-to-heaven language fulfils the text without physical bodies.","sources":["king-spirit-of-prophecy","preston-eschatology-org","wright-resurrection-son-god","mathison-when-shall"]}],"sources":["hoekema-bible-future","ladd-blessed-hope","wright-resurrection-son-god"]},{"id":"acts-1-6-11-restore-the-kingdom","title":"Will You Restore the Kingdom to Israel?","ref":"Acts 1:6–11","ranges":["Acts 1:6-11"],"category":"acts","summary":"The disciples ask the risen Jesus whether he will now restore the kingdom to Israel. He replies that it is not for them to know the times or seasons the Father has fixed, but they will receive power when the Holy Spirit comes and be his witnesses in Jerusalem, Judea, Samaria and to the end of the earth. He is taken up and a cloud hides him; two men in white say this same Jesus will come in the same way as they saw him go.","keyIssue":"Does Jesus affirm a future restoration of national Israel (only the timing is withheld), or redirect the question so that the kingdom is restored through the Spirit-empowered witness to the nations? And what does 'in the same way' imply about the manner of his return?","timeText":false,"topics":["israel","kingdom","second-coming"],"readings":[{"position":"disppremil","reading":"Jesus does not correct the disciples' expectation of a restored kingdom for Israel; he corrects only their curiosity about timing. The kingdom will be restored to Israel at his return, which will be personal, bodily and visible to the Mount of Olives (1:11–12; Zechariah 14:4), after the present church age of witness.","support":"After forty days of teaching about the kingdom (1:3), the apostles' question is assumed to be informed, not confused; 'times or seasons' implies there is a time; Acts 3:21 and Luke 22:29–30 keep Israel's restoration in view.","objection":"Covenant theologians respond that Jesus's answer replaces the national-political horizon with a universal one (1:8), and that the Spirit's coming, which the prophets tied to Israel's restoration (Isaiah 32:15; 49:6), is the beginning of that restoration.","sources":["vlach-he-will-reign-forever","saucy-progressive-dispensationalism","bock-acts-becnt"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"Jesus redirects the question: Israel's restoration is being accomplished now as the Spirit is poured out and the witness goes from Jerusalem to the ends of the earth, fulfilling Isaiah 49:6, where restoring Jacob and being a light to the nations are one task. The return 'in the same way' is Christ's personal, visible Second Coming at the end of the age.","support":"Acts 1:8 echoes Isaiah 49:6 and 32:15; Acts 15:16–18 interprets the rebuilt tent of David as the inclusion of Gentiles; the gathered remnant of Jews in Acts 2 is the restored Israel's nucleus.","objection":"Dispensationalists respond that this reads Isaiah selectively and that Jesus's answer would be misleading if the disciples' premise was wrong; amillennialists answer that Jesus regularly reframed questions (e.g. John 3:4).","sources":["riddlebarger-case-amil","beale-temple-church-mission","hoekema-bible-future"]},{"position":"postmil","reading":"The witness 'to the end of the earth' is the program by which Christ's kingdom expands until it fills the world; his return 'in the same way' is the final, bodily, visible Second Coming after that expansion. Postmillennial partial preterists such as Gentry insist that Acts 1:11 is not the AD 70 coming but the future Parousia.","support":"The parallel of ascension in a cloud and return in a cloud, the stress on 'this same Jesus', and the eyewitness emphasis ('as you saw him go') all point to a personal, visible return.","objection":"Amillennialists accept the bodily return but question the expected scale of success before it; full preterists object that the same 'cloud coming' language in the Gospels is applied to AD 70 by partial preterists themselves.","sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","gentry-pmw-hyper-preterism","mathison-postmillennialism"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"The return 'in the same way' means 'in a cloud', as the Old Testament describes the LORD coming in clouds of judgment; Christ returned in that manner in AD 70, discerned by faith rather than by physical sight. Restoration of the kingdom to Israel was the establishment of the new covenant people.","support":"Consistency with the AD 70 interpretation of 'coming on the clouds' in Matthew 24:30 and 26:64, which partial preterists themselves accept.","objection":"Critics argue that 'this same Jesus' and 'in the same way as you saw him go' stress a visible, bodily departure and return; denying a future bodily return places the view outside historic creedal orthodoxy.","sources":["preston-eschatology-org","king-spirit-of-prophecy","mathison-when-shall"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"The church's mission and the Spirit's coming begin the restoration hope, while the bodily return of Christ will establish his reign on earth for the millennium; whether ethnic Israel has a special place in that reign is debated within the tradition.","support":"Keeps both the redirection of 1:8 and the literal return of 1:11, without requiring a pretribulational framework.","objection":"Dispensationalists see too little emphasis on Israel; amillennialists see no need for a millennium between the return and the new creation.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","blomberg-chung-historic-premil"]}],"sources":["bock-acts-becnt","riddlebarger-case-amil","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"]},{"id":"acts-3-19-21-restoration-of-all-things","title":"Times of Refreshing and the Restoration of All Things","ref":"Acts 3:19–21","ranges":["Acts 3:19-21"],"category":"acts","summary":"Peter calls the people of Jerusalem to repent so that their sins may be blotted out, 'times of refreshing' may come from the Lord's presence, and he may send the Christ appointed for them, Jesus, whom heaven must receive until the time of the restoration of all things (apokatastasis pantōn) spoken of by the prophets.","keyIssue":"Is the 'restoration of all things' a millennial restoration of Israel's kingdom triggered by national repentance, the progressive subduing of Christ's enemies while he reigns from heaven, or the final new creation at his return?","timeText":false,"topics":["israel","kingdom","second-coming","optimism"],"readings":[{"position":"disppremil","reading":"Peter re-offers the kingdom to Israel: if the nation repents, Messiah will return and bring the restoration the prophets foretold, the millennial kingdom with Israel at its centre. Israel's rejection postponed this, and the restoration awaits national repentance at the end of the Tribulation.","support":"Acts 3:20 says Jesus was appointed 'for you' (Israel); 'restoration' echoes the disciples' question in Acts 1:6 (the verb apokathistēmi); the prophets' restoration oracles centre on Israel's land and kingdom.","objection":"Non-dispensationalists question whether the kingdom was 'offered' and 'postponed', and note that Peter immediately applies the promise to Abraham's blessing of 'all the families of the earth' (3:25–26).","sources":["saucy-progressive-dispensationalism","bock-acts-becnt","pentecost-things-to-come"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"The restoration of all things is the consummated new creation at Christ's return. Christ remains in heaven, reigning, until that day; when he comes, the restoration is complete. This leaves no room for an earthly millennial reign after his return before the final state.","support":"'Heaven must receive him until' sets the Second Coming as the moment of restoration; Romans 8:19–23 and Revelation 21:5 ('I make all things new') describe the cosmic renewal the prophets foresaw (Isaiah 65:17).","objection":"Premillennialists respond that a millennium is the first stage of the restoration and the new creation its completion; postmillennialists stress that the restoration is in progress before the return.","sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil","riddlebarger-isaiah-65"]},{"position":"postmil","reading":"Christ must stay in heaven until the restoration is accomplished: he rules from his throne now, subduing his enemies through the gospel (Psalm 110:1; 1 Corinthians 15:25), and returns only when that work has run its course. The passage thus implies that the restoration is a historical process brought to completion before the Second Coming.","support":"Psalm 110:1 ('sit at my right hand until I make your enemies your footstool') and 1 Corinthians 15:24–26 together place the subduing of enemies during Christ's heavenly session, before his return.","objection":"Amillennialists answer that 'until the restoration' may mean 'until the time when restoration happens', at the return itself, not after a process; premillennialists say Psalm 110 does not specify the means of subjection.","sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","bahnsen-victory-in-jesus","boettner-millennium"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"The restoration begins at Christ's return with his reign over a renewed earth, the millennium, and is completed in the new heavens and new earth; repentance brings present 'refreshing' through the Spirit as a foretaste.","support":"Coheres with Acts 1:6 and Romans 11's hope for Israel without the postponement theory; the prophets whose restoration Peter cites (3:21) picture a renewed earth with Israel and the nations at peace (Isaiah 11; 65), which premillennialists see beginning visibly at the return.","objection":"Amillennialists argue that the restoration of 'all things' at the return is total, not partial.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","grudem-systematic"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"The restoration of all things was completed when the old covenant world passed and the new covenant order was fully established in AD 70; the 'times of refreshing' are the new covenant life now available in Christ.","support":"Peter speaks to his own generation; Jesus said Elijah 'restores all things' (Matthew 17:11) and connected the end with that generation.","objection":"Critics note the language of creation-wide restoration (Romans 8:21) has no clear fulfilment in AD 70, and the view's denial of a future return is outside the historic creeds.","sources":["preston-eschatology-org","mathison-when-shall"]}],"sources":["bock-acts-becnt","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","hoekema-bible-future"]},{"id":"2-peter-3-day-of-the-lord","title":"Scoffers, Delay and New Heavens and Earth","ref":"2 Peter 3:1–13","ranges":["2 Peter 3:1-13"],"category":"epistles","summary":"Peter warns that scoffers will come in the last days asking where the promise of Christ's coming is. He answers that as the world was once destroyed by water, so the present heavens and earth are stored up for fire; that with the Lord a day is as a thousand years; that the delay is patience; and that the day of the Lord will come like a thief, when the heavens pass away with a roar, the elements (stoicheia) dissolve with heat and the earth and its works 'will be found' (or 'burned up', a textual variant). Believers wait for new heavens and a new earth where righteousness dwells.","keyIssue":"Is this the final conflagration and renewal of the physical cosmos at the Second Coming, or covenantal language for the end of the old covenant order in AD 70? And does the fire annihilate creation or purge and renew it?","timeText":false,"topics":["second-coming","final-judgment","kingdom"],"readings":[{"position":"amil","reading":"The final Day of the Lord at Christ's return, when the present cosmos is judged and purified by fire and renewed as the new heavens and new earth; the delay is God's patience during the whole age. Most Reformed amillennialists read 3:10 'will be found' (heurethēsetai) as laying bare for judgment, and the fire as purifying rather than annihilating.","support":"The parallel with the Flood (3:5–7), which judged but did not annihilate the world, points to cosmic renewal; Romans 8:21 and Revelation 21:1 confirm the hope; 'a thousand years as a day' assumes a long delay is possible.","objection":"Partial and full preterists argue that 'heavens and earth' can be covenantal-political language (Isaiah 51:15–16), and that stoicheia elsewhere means the elementary principles of the old order (Galatians 4:3, 9; Colossians 2:8, 20).","sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil","middleton-new-heaven-earth"]},{"position":"postmil","reading":"Many postmillennialists, including Gentry and Mathison, read 3:10–13 as the final consummation at Christ's single bodily return, after the long era of gospel advance; the 'delay' allows the gospel to prosper for a long age. Peter's appeal to creation and the Flood points to the material cosmos, not merely the Jewish order.","support":"Peter grounds his argument in the physical creation and the global Flood (3:5–6), and the new heavens and earth are a place 'where righteousness dwells', a consummate state (Isaiah 65:17 language here applied to its final form).","objection":"Other postmillennial preterists counter that the 'last days' and scoffers fit the generation before AD 70 (Matthew 24:48); Gentry answers that Peter's anticipating a long time span ('a thousand years as a day') points beyond that generation.","sources":["gentry-2-peter-3-consummation","mathison-postmillennialism","mathison-when-shall"]},{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"Some partial preterists (e.g. Leithart) read the passing 'heavens and earth' as the old covenant world, with its temple and elemental ordinances, dissolved in AD 70; the new heavens and new earth are the new covenant order. John Owen, in a sermon on 3:11, had already applied the dissolving heavens and earth to the Jewish church-state.","support":"Isaiah uses 'heavens and earth' for the covenant order (Isaiah 51:16; 65:17 is followed by a world still containing death, 65:20); 'the last days' (3:3) refers to the end of the old covenant age (Hebrews 1:2; 9:26); stoicheia means elementary religious principles elsewhere in Paul.","objection":"Other partial preterists, such as Gentry, reject this reading because Peter compares the coming judgment with the Flood and creation itself. Critics also note that the passage appears in a letter that expects a long delay, the opposite of the near-coming emphasis in Matthew 24.","sources":["leithart-promise-appearing","owen-providential-changes","gentry-2-peter-3-consummation"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"The passage describes the passing of the old covenant world in AD 70; the new heavens and new earth of 3:13 are the new covenant realm in which righteousness dwells now, and there is no future cosmic conflagration.","support":"Don Preston argues that Peter's 'promise' comes from the prophets (3:2), whose new creation oracles (Isaiah 65–66) concerned Israel's covenant world, and that Jesus limited 'heaven and earth passing' to his generation (Matthew 24:34–35).","objection":"Critics, including partial preterists, argue that Peter's cosmic Flood analogy, the global judgment of 'the ungodly' and the scope of the new creation exceed AD 70; the view's denial of a future Second Coming and judgment places it outside historic creedal orthodoxy.","sources":["preston-elements-shall-melt","king-spirit-of-prophecy","gentry-pmw-hyper-preterism"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"The Day of the Lord is an extended period beginning with the Tribulation and running through the millennium; the dissolution of the heavens and earth by fire comes at its end, after the millennium and the final judgment, preparing for the eternal state of Revelation 21.","support":"'A day is as a thousand years' (3:8) is sometimes linked to the thousand-year span of the Day of the Lord; Revelation 20:11–21:1 places the passing of heaven and earth after the millennium.","objection":"Critics say that stretching 'the day' across a thousand years blurs Peter's point that the day comes suddenly, 'like a thief' (3:10).","sources":["walvoord-millennial-kingdom","pentecost-things-to-come"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"The final conflagration and renewal follow the millennial reign of Christ and the last judgment; Peter does not mention the millennium because his concern is the certainty of the final day against scoffers.","support":"Fits the sequence of Revelation 20–21 while reading 2 Peter 3 as the final state.","objection":"Amillennialists argue that 2 Peter 3 links Christ's coming directly to the cosmic renewal, leaving no space for an intervening millennium.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","bauckham-jude-2-peter"]}],"sources":["gentry-2-peter-3-consummation","bauckham-jude-2-peter","hoekema-bible-future","preston-elements-shall-melt"]},{"id":"hebrews-8-13-ready-to-vanish","title":"Old Covenant Ready to Vanish Away","ref":"Hebrews 8:13","ranges":["Hebrews 8:13"],"category":"epistles","summary":"After quoting Jeremiah 31:31–34 at length, the author says that by speaking of a 'new' covenant God has made the first obsolete, and what is becoming obsolete and growing old is near (engys) to disappearing.","keyIssue":"If the old covenant was made obsolete at the cross, why is it still 'near to disappearing' when Hebrews is written? Does this point to the temple's destruction in AD 70 as the visible end of the old covenant order, and what follows for eschatology?","timeText":true,"topics":["olivet-generation","israel","kingdom"],"readings":[{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"The old covenant was made obsolete in principle by Christ's death, but its institutions, the temple and sacrifices, were still standing and about to vanish; that happened in AD 70. Hebrews thus stands in a forty-year 'transition generation' between the old and new covenant eras, and it supports reading the 'last days' as the end of the old covenant age (Hebrews 1:2; 9:26).","support":"The present tense of the participles ('becoming obsolete and growing old') and engys ('near') imply an imminent historical event; the letter assumes the sacrifices still continue (10:1–3), and many date it before 70.","objection":"Some amillennialists respond that the verse may describe the inherent temporariness of the old order rather than predict AD 70. Preterists answer that the author's 'yet a little while' (10:37) and 'the Day drawing near' (10:25) supply the timeframe.","sources":["gentry-before-jerusalem-fell","demar-last-days-madness","lane-hebrews-wbc"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"The covenant transition, begun at the cross, was completed only in AD 70, when the old covenant world passed; that moment was the arrival of the 'age to come', the resurrection and the Parousia (9:28). Since the old covenant vanished then, all eschatological promises tied to it were fulfilled then as well.","support":"The 'nearness' of 8:13 combined with 'he who is coming will come and will not delay' (10:37) and Christ appearing a 'second time' (9:28) are read as one event.","objection":"Critics accept that 8:13 points to AD 70 but deny that the end of the temple equals the bodily resurrection and final judgment; they note that Hebrews 9:27–28 links the second appearing to the judgment after death. The denial of a future return places the view outside historic creedal orthodoxy.","sources":["king-cross-parousia","preston-eschatology-org","mathison-when-shall"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"The old covenant became obsolete with Christ's once-for-all sacrifice, and the passing of its shadows was sealed by the destruction of the temple. This does not end the age: the church lives in the 'last days' between Christ's first and second comings and awaits the city to come (13:14).","support":"Hebrews 9:9–10 calls the old system a symbol 'until the time of reformation'; Lane and others date Hebrews in the 60s and see 8:13 as alert to the temple's coming end.","objection":"Partial preterists press that the amillennial view underplays how much New Testament 'nearness' language is tied to AD 70; amillennialists answer that a near judgment does not exhaust the hope of the age to come.","sources":["lane-hebrews-wbc","vos-pauline-eschatology","hoekema-bible-future"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"The Mosaic covenant as a rule of life ended at the cross; the new covenant, made with Israel and Judah, is now applied spiritually to the church, but it will be fulfilled nationally with Israel at Christ's return. The obsolescence of the old covenant does not imply that God's promises to national Israel are cancelled.","support":"Jeremiah 31's new covenant is addressed to 'the house of Israel and the house of Judah'; progressive dispensationalists hold one new covenant with present (church) and future (Israel) phases.","objection":"Critics ask how this squares with a millennial temple and sacrifices (Ezekiel 40–48) after the old covenant has 'vanished'. Dispensationalists answer that millennial worship belongs to the new covenant, not a revival of the Mosaic covenant.","sources":["ryrie-dispensationalism","blaising-bock-progressive","saucy-progressive-dispensationalism"]},{"position":"postmil","reading":"The decisive end of the old covenant in AD 70 vindicated Christ and cleared the stage for the new covenant kingdom to advance among the nations; the 'better covenant' (8:6) with its law written on hearts is the engine of the gospel's progressive success.","support":"Jeremiah 31:34 promises that 'all shall know me, from the least to the greatest', which postmillennialists take as an expanding reality as the new covenant spreads.","objection":"Amillennialists read 'all shall know me' as describing every member of the new covenant community, not an eventual majority of humankind.","sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","mathison-postmillennialism"]}],"sources":["lane-hebrews-wbc","gentry-before-jerusalem-fell","king-cross-parousia"]},{"id":"hebrews-12-22-28-unshakable-kingdom","title":"Mount Zion and the Unshakable Kingdom","ref":"Hebrews 12:22–28","ranges":["Hebrews 12:22-28"],"category":"epistles","summary":"Believers 'have come' not to Sinai but to Mount Zion, the heavenly Jerusalem, to innumerable angels, the assembly of the firstborn, God the judge, the spirits of the righteous made perfect, and Jesus the mediator of a new covenant. God promises, quoting Haggai 2:6, 'yet once more' to shake not only the earth but heaven, removing what is shaken so that what cannot be shaken may remain; since we are receiving (present participle) an unshakable kingdom, we should worship with reverence.","keyIssue":"Is the 'shaking' of heaven and earth the end of the old covenant order in AD 70, the final cosmic judgment, or both? And how does the present 'have come to Zion' relate to future hope?","timeText":false,"topics":["kingdom","second-coming","israel"],"readings":[{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"The 'shaking' that removes what is made is the dismantling of the old covenant order, climaxing in AD 70, while the church is already receiving the unshakable kingdom. Haggai 2:6–7 was originally about the shaking of nations and the glory of the latter temple, which the author sees fulfilled in the new covenant.","support":"'Receiving' is present tense; 8:13 says the old order is near to disappearing; the contrast throughout chapter 12 is between Sinai and Zion, two covenants, not two cosmic eras.","objection":"Critics note that 12:26 says God will shake 'heaven' as well as earth, and that 12:27 speaks of removing 'created things', which suggests the final cosmic judgment. Partial preterists answer that the language is covenantal, as in the prophets.","sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","demar-last-days-madness","owen-providential-changes"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"An inaugurated eschatology: believers have already come to heavenly Zion, joined to the assembly in heaven, while the final shaking of heaven and earth at Christ's return will remove the present created order in its fallen form and leave the unshakable kingdom fully revealed. AD 70 may be a foreshadowing, but the 'yet once more' points to the consummation.","support":"'Yet once more' (12:27) signals a final, once-for-all event; Hebrews elsewhere locates the believer's hope in a city 'to come' (13:14) and a 'second appearing' (9:28); Vos saw Hebrews as the classic expression of the 'already' heavenly participation.","objection":"Preterists argue that 'shaking' language in Haggai referred to historical upheaval among nations; amillennialists respond that Hebrews deliberately widens it to heaven and earth.","sources":["vos-pauline-eschatology","lane-hebrews-wbc","hoekema-bible-future"]},{"position":"postmil","reading":"The present reception of the unshakable kingdom guarantees its triumph: what is shaken (the old order and every opposing power) is removed, while Christ's kingdom remains and advances until all is subject to him; the passage grounds confidence, not retreat.","support":"The removal of the old and the permanence of the new fit Daniel 2:44 (a kingdom that will never be destroyed) and Christ's present reign (Hebrews 1:13; 10:12–13, 'waiting until his enemies are made a footstool').","objection":"Amillennialists agree that the kingdom is unshakable but deny that this entails visible historical dominance before the end.","sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","mathison-postmillennialism"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"The heavenly Jerusalem is the present spiritual position of the church and the future dwelling of the saints (Revelation 21:2), distinct from the earthly Jerusalem of the millennium; the final shaking occurs at the end of the age, through the Tribulation judgments and the passing of heaven and earth. The passage does not replace Israel's earthly Zion promises.","support":"Hebrews speaks of a heavenly city, which dispensationalists distinguish from the earthly city promised to Israel (Zechariah 14; Isaiah 2).","objection":"Covenant theologians reply that the author contrasts the earthly Sinai with the heavenly Zion precisely to wean readers from earthly Jerusalem (13:12–14).","sources":["vlach-he-will-reign-forever","pentecost-things-to-come"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"The shaking was entirely completed in AD 70; the unshakable kingdom fully came then, and believers now dwell in the heavenly Jerusalem with no further cosmic shaking to come.","support":"'Yet a little while' (10:37) and the near disappearance of the old (8:13) fix the timeframe. Proponents add that the 'things that have been made' which are removed (12:27) echo Hebrews' language for the handmade earthly sanctuary (9:11, 24), pointing to the temple order rather than the physical cosmos.","objection":"Critics object that the passage contrasts the created order with what is unshakable, a cosmic distinction that AD 70 did not effect; the denial of a future consummation places the view outside historic creedal orthodoxy.","sources":["king-cross-parousia","mathison-when-shall"]}],"sources":["lane-hebrews-wbc","vos-pauline-eschatology","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"]},{"id":"2-timothy-3-1-5-perilous-times","title":"Perilous Times in the Last Days","ref":"2 Timothy 3:1–5","ranges":["2 Timothy 3:1-5"],"category":"epistles","summary":"Paul tells Timothy that in the last days difficult (chalepoi) times will come: people will be lovers of self and money, proud, abusive, disobedient to parents, ungrateful, unholy, heartless, lovers of pleasure rather than God, having a form of godliness but denying its power. 'Avoid such people', he adds (3:5); such teachers will 'not get very far', for their folly will be plain (3:9).","keyIssue":"Is this a forecast of moral decline at the end of the church age, a description of the whole interadvent era, or a picture of the apostolic generation before AD 70? What does it imply for optimism or pessimism about history?","timeText":false,"topics":["optimism","church-history","antichrist"],"readings":[{"position":"disppremil","reading":"A prophecy of the moral and spiritual decline that will characterize the end of the church age, especially in professing Christendom, as part of the apostasy before the Lord's return (2 Thessalonians 2:3; 1 Timothy 4:1). Many see present cultural trends as a sign that the end is near.","support":"Paul's future tenses ('will come', 'will be'); parallels in Matthew 24:12 and Luke 18:8; the description of religious form without power fits an apostate church.","objection":"Critics note that Paul tells Timothy to avoid such people (3:5) and describes them as already present in Ephesus (3:6–8), which shows the 'last days' include Timothy's day. Dispensationalists reply that present instances foreshadow an intensified final form.","sources":["walvoord-millennial-kingdom","ryrie-dispensationalism"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"The 'last days' span the whole period from Christ's first coming to his return (Acts 2:17; Hebrews 1:2; 1 John 2:18); the vices Paul lists recur throughout church history and may intensify near the end, but they mark the age as a whole rather than one final phase. Believers face them with Scripture (3:14–17), not alarm.","support":"Paul applies the warning to Timothy's present ministry (3:5–9); Towner and others read the 'last days' as already under way in Ephesus.","objection":"Dispensationalists argue that the future tense points beyond Timothy's day; postmillennialists ask whether the amillennial reading leaves room for real cultural progress.","sources":["hoekema-bible-future","towner-timothy-titus","riddlebarger-case-amil"]},{"position":"postmil","reading":"The 'last days' are the whole new covenant era, and Paul describes 'seasons' (kairoi) of difficulty within it, not unrelieved decline. Crucially, 3:9 says such people 'will not get very far' because their folly will be exposed: error is real but self-limiting, while the gospel advances.","support":"The plural kairoi suggests recurring periods; 3:9 limits the progress of evil; the context sends Timothy to preach the word, which equips for every good work (3:16–4:2).","objection":"Amillennialists and premillennialists see 3:13 ('evil people and impostors will go on from bad to worse') as signalling persistent or growing evil; postmillennialists answer that 3:13 describes the impostors' own trajectory, not society's overall direction.","sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","mathison-postmillennialism","boettner-millennium"]},{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"The 'last days' are the final days of the old covenant age, the forty years from the cross to AD 70; Paul warns Timothy about conditions in the church and society of his own generation, which climaxed in the apostasy and judgment Jesus foretold (Matthew 24:10–12).","support":"Paul instructs Timothy personally to avoid these people; the description matches the turmoil of the 60s; Hebrews 1:2 and 1 Peter 1:20 place the 'last times' in the apostolic era.","objection":"Amillennialists accept the present application but argue the last days continue until Christ's return (1 Corinthians 10:11; Hebrews 9:26 speaks of the 'end of the ages' as begun, not ended).","sources":["demar-last-days-madness","gentry-before-jerusalem-fell"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"The last days began with Christ, and the vices of 3:1–5 will mark the whole age, culminating in a final apostasy and the Antichrist before Christ returns to establish his kingdom; the passage calls for vigilance throughout.","support":"Combines the present application (3:5–9) with the future intensification pointed to in 2 Thessalonians 2.","objection":"Postmillennialists object that this assumes a final decline the text does not require.","sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","grudem-systematic"]}],"sources":["towner-timothy-titus","hoekema-bible-future","demar-last-days-madness","walvoord-millennial-kingdom"]},{"id":"revelation-1-and-22-time-texts","title":"Things That Must Soon Take Place","ref":"Revelation 1:1–3; 22:6–10","ranges":["Revelation 1:1-3","Revelation 22:6-10"],"category":"revelation","summary":"Revelation opens and closes with time markers: it reveals 'the things that must soon (en tachei) take place' (1:1; 22:6), 'the time is near' (engys, 1:3; 22:10), and John is told not to seal up the words of the prophecy (22:10), in contrast to Daniel, who was told to seal his vision because it concerned a distant time (Daniel 8:26; 12:4, 9). Jesus says 'I am coming soon' (22:7, 12, 20).","keyIssue":"How should 'soon' and 'near' govern the interpretation of the book? They are the central texts for preterism, and every school must give an account of them.","timeText":true,"topics":["revelation-date","olivet-generation","second-coming"],"readings":[{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"The time markers are meant literally and frame the book: most of Revelation's prophecies (especially chs. 4–19) were fulfilled soon after writing, in the judgment on Jerusalem in AD 70 and the Roman crisis around it. On the early date (c. 65–66) that most partial preterists hold, 'soon' meant within a few years. Chapters 20–22 extend to the present age and the final consummation.","support":"The markers stand at both ends of the book as bookends; the contrast with Daniel's sealing (Daniel 12:4) shows the time had arrived; the first readers were seven real churches promised blessing for heeding what was near (1:3).","objection":"Critics ask why the time texts should be strict for chapters 4–19 but not for 20–22, which also lie within the bracketing statements. Partial preterists answer that the book itself signals long-term developments (a 'thousand years', 20:2–7). The date of Revelation is also disputed (pre-70 vs c. 95).","sources":["gentry-before-jerusalem-fell","chilton-days-vengeance","mathison-preterist-approach-revelation"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"Because the same time markers bracket the whole book, everything in it, including the Second Coming, resurrection, judgment and new creation of chs. 19–22, was fulfilled by AD 70. Consistency forbids splitting the book, as partial preterists do.","support":"Jesus says 'I am coming soon' in chapter 22 itself (22:7, 12, 20), right alongside the new Jerusalem visions; J. Stuart Russell's The Parousia pressed this consistency argument.","objection":"Critics reply that 'coming soon' language can describe impending comings in judgment within history (as in 2:5, 16) without being the final coming, and that the denial of a future bodily return and resurrection places the view outside historic creedal orthodoxy.","sources":["russell-parousia","king-spirit-of-prophecy","mathison-when-shall"]},{"position":"futurism","reading":"'En tachei' can mean 'quickly' or 'suddenly', describing the rapidity of the events when they begin, not their nearness; and 'near' is measured from God's perspective, for whom a thousand years is as a day (2 Peter 3:8). The events of chs. 4–22 lie mostly in the future, and the church is to live in imminent expectation.","support":"The same phrase en tachei is used of rapid action in Luke 18:8 and Acts 12:7; the New Testament treats the Lord's return as imminent yet not dated; the book's scope (global judgments, Satan's binding, new creation) exceeds any first-century event.","objection":"Preterists reply that 'near' (engys) is not a word about manner, and that a two-thousand-year delay strips the time markers of meaning for the first readers. Futurists answer that imminence is real: the next event on God's calendar could come at any time.","sources":["walvoord-revelation-jesus-christ","thomas-revelation-8-22","osborne-revelation-becnt"]},{"position":"historicism","reading":"The prophecies began to be fulfilled soon after John wrote and then unfold in sequence through church history, from imperial Rome through the fall of the empire, the rise of the papacy and Islam, to the end; 'soon' refers to the beginning of the chain, which started in John's day.","support":"Accounts for the time markers without collapsing the book into one generation, and gives the book relevance to every era of history as a continuous prophecy.","objection":"Critics note that historicist schemes have disagreed widely and repeatedly had to be revised as predicted end dates passed, and that the first readers could hardly identify fulfilments centuries later.","sources":["elliott-horae","barnes-notes-revelation"]},{"position":"idealism","reading":"'Soon' expresses the inaugurated nature of the last days: with Christ's death and resurrection, the latter days of Daniel have begun (Revelation 1:1 echoes Daniel 2:28–29, replacing 'in the latter days' with 'soon'). The book's symbols describe the conflict of Christ and Satan throughout the whole age, every part of which is 'near' to the end.","support":"Beale notes that Revelation 1:1 alludes to Daniel 2 and changes 'latter days' to 'soon', implying that Daniel's kingdom prophecy has begun to be fulfilled; Hendriksen's 'progressive parallelism' reads each cycle as covering the whole age.","objection":"Preterists say this dissolves concrete time markers into a general nearness; futurists say it loses the book's prophetic specificity.","sources":["beale-revelation-nigtc","hendriksen-more-than-conquerors","poythress-returning-king"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"The book addressed real first-century crises and promised that the decisive events had begun, but its climax, Christ's coming, the millennium and the new creation, remains future; prophetic perspective telescopes near and far events, as the Old Testament prophets did.","support":"Ladd combined preterist and futurist elements: the beast of chapter 13 is both Rome and the end-time Antichrist.","objection":"Preterists see the telescoping theory as an escape; idealists see it as unnecessarily concrete.","sources":["ladd-commentary-revelation","gregg-revelation-four-views"]}],"sources":["gregg-revelation-four-views","gentry-before-jerusalem-fell","walvoord-revelation-jesus-christ","beale-revelation-nigtc"]},{"id":"revelation-13-beast-666","title":"The Beasts of Revelation 13 and the Number 666","ref":"Revelation 13","ranges":["Revelation 13:1-18"],"category":"revelation","summary":"A beast with ten horns and seven heads rises from the sea; the dragon gives it his power and throne. One head seems mortally wounded but is healed, and the whole earth marvels. It blasphemes and makes war on the saints for forty-two months. A second beast, from the land, with lamb-like horns but a dragon's voice (later called the false prophet, 19:20), performs signs, causes an image of the first beast to be worshipped, and requires a mark on the right hand or forehead for buying and selling. The beast's number is 'the number of a man': 666 (13:18), though some ancient witnesses read 616.","keyIssue":"Who or what is the beast: Nero and the Roman Empire, the papacy, a future Antichrist, or the persecuting state in every age? How does the number 666, and the variant 616, fit each view?","timeText":false,"topics":["antichrist","great-tribulation","revelation-date"],"readings":[{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"The sea beast is the Roman Empire and, as one of its heads, Nero Caesar, the first emperor to persecute Christians (AD 64–68), whose campaign lasted about forty-two months (late 64 to June 68). The number confirms it: spelled in Hebrew letters as nrwn qsr (Nero Caesar), the name totals 666 (50+200+6+50+100+60+200); in the Latin form Nero, without the final n (50), it totals 616, which explains the ancient variant. The land beast is a local power promoting emperor worship, variously the provincial imperial cult in Asia or (Chilton) Jerusalem's leadership collaborating with Rome.","support":"The spelling nrwn qsr is attested in an Aramaic document from Wadi Murabba'at (Hillers), and the 616 variant, known to Irenaeus and found in P115 (the oldest papyrus of Revelation 13) and Codex Ephraemi, is best explained by a scribe adjusting for the Latin spelling. Revelation calls the number one 'for one who has understanding' (13:18), a puzzle the first readers could solve. Bauckham, not a preterist, also accepts the Nero gematria.","objection":"Critics note that Irenaeus, who knew the 616 variant, never suggested Nero; that a Greek-speaking audience is asked to compute in Hebrew letters; and that the Hebrew form 'qsr' is a defective spelling. Proponents answer that John's Greek is full of Hebrew idiom (e.g. Abaddon, 9:11; Armageddon, 16:16), and that the Murabba'at text shows the spelling was in use.","sources":["gentry-beast-of-revelation","gentry-pmw-beasts-of-revelation","bauckham-climax-prophecy","hillers-murabbaat","chilton-days-vengeance"]},{"position":"idealism","reading":"The sea beast is the persecuting political power of every age, with Rome as John's present example; the land beast is false religion and propaganda that serves it. 666 is symbolic: six falls short of seven (completeness), so triple six signifies the beast's persistent failure to be divine despite its imitation of the Trinity. Many idealists allow that John may have used a Nero gematria as a pointer to the type.","support":"The dragon, sea beast and land beast form a parody of Father, Son and Spirit; the beast's 'death wound' parodies the slain Lamb (5:6; 13:3); John's numbers elsewhere are symbolic (7, 12, 144,000). Bauckham notes 666 is a triangular number (the sum of 1 to 36) and that 'beast' (thērion) spelled in Hebrew also totals 666.","objection":"Preterists argue that 'the number of a man' (13:18) calls for an individual identification; futurists say the symbolic reading empties the specific details (a mark, an image, a forty-two-month reign).","sources":["hendriksen-more-than-conquerors","beale-revelation-nigtc","poythress-returning-king","bauckham-climax-prophecy"]},{"position":"futurism","reading":"The sea beast is a future world ruler, the Antichrist, who heads a revived form of the Roman Empire (ten horns, Daniel 7:7–8, 24) and persecutes believers for the last three and a half years of Daniel's seventieth week. The healed head may be a literal recovery from apparent death or a revived empire; the land beast is a future religious leader; the mark is a literal means of economic control. The number will identify the man when he appears; past calculations have been premature.","support":"The beast's universal worship (13:8), its specific forty-two-month span matching Daniel 7:25 and 12:7, and its destruction at Christ's return (19:19–20) point to events not yet fulfilled; 2 Thessalonians 2:3–8 describes a future 'man of lawlessness'.","objection":"Preterists and idealists reply that John's first readers were told to calculate the number (13:18), which would be meaningless if it concerned someone two thousand years later; futurists answer that the instruction is addressed to all who read the prophecy, including the last generation.","sources":["walvoord-revelation-jesus-christ","thomas-revelation-8-22","pentecost-things-to-come"]},{"position":"historicism","reading":"The sea beast is the papacy as a politico-religious power that arose from the fallen Roman Empire; the forty-two months are 1,260 years on the day-for-a-year principle (often dated 538–1798, from Justinian's era to the French capture of Pope Pius VI). The land beast is the papal hierarchy or, in some versions, a later power. 666 has been read as Lateinos (Greek for 'the Latin [man/kingdom]', 30+1+300+5+10+50+70+200 = 666), a suggestion first made by Irenaeus and favoured by Elliott; Adventist writers popularized the title 'Vicarius Filii Dei'.","support":"Luther, Calvin and many later Protestant commentators identified the papacy with the Antichrist powers of Daniel 7 and Revelation 13; Daniel 7's little horn rising after the fourth empire fits a power arising from Rome; the computation in Lateinos goes back to Irenaeus.","objection":"Critics note that 'Vicarius Filii Dei' is not an official papal title, that many different names can be made to total 666, and that the 1,260-year dates shift between historicist writers. Historicists answer that the convergence of the chronology with the history of papal temporal power is striking.","sources":["elliott-horae","barnes-notes-revelation","irenaeus-against-heresies"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"The beast is both Rome in John's day and the eschatological Antichrist in whom the persecuting power will culminate before Christ's return; Ladd saw Nero redivivus legends in the background and held that the fulfilment is still future. The number may allude to Nero as a type of the final Antichrist.","support":"Reflects how biblical prophecy often has a near historical referent and a future culmination (compare Antiochus Epiphanes in Daniel 11).","objection":"Preterists consider the future extension unnecessary; futurists consider the Roman reference too strong a concession.","sources":["ladd-commentary-revelation","bauckham-climax-prophecy"]},{"position":"postmil","reading":"Postmillennialists mostly follow the preterist identification of the beast with Nero and Rome: the beast was defeated in the first century, Christ's kingdom triumphed over imperial Rome, and the church need not fear a future world dictator. Others, such as James Jordan and Peter Leithart, propose alternative backgrounds for 666, notably the 666 talents of gold Solomon received yearly (1 Kings 10:14), which marks a king corrupted by wealth and power.","support":"The Solomonic reading fits the economic stress of 13:16–17 and the Old Testament background John uses everywhere; it is offered alongside or instead of the Nero calculation.","objection":"Critics of the Solomon reading note that 1 Kings 10:14 involves talents, not a man's name, whereas 13:18 calls it the number of a man and a name (13:17). Futurists add that a first-century fulfilment cannot account for the beast's final destruction at Christ's return (19:19–20).","sources":["leithart-beastly-number","gentry-before-jerusalem-fell","williams-p115-number-beast"]}],"sources":["bauckham-climax-prophecy","williams-p115-number-beast","gentry-beast-of-revelation","walvoord-revelation-jesus-christ","aune-revelation-wbc"]},{"id":"revelation-17-harlot-seven-kings","title":"The Harlot, the Seven Hills and the Seven Kings","ref":"Revelation 17","ranges":["Revelation 17:1-18"],"category":"revelation","summary":"An angel shows John a woman, 'Babylon the great, mother of prostitutes', drunk with the blood of the saints, sitting on a scarlet beast with seven heads and ten horns. The angel explains that the seven heads are seven mountains on which the woman sits and also seven kings: 'five have fallen, one is, the other has not yet come, and when he comes he must remain a little while'; the beast that was and is not is an eighth, yet 'of the seven' (17:9–11). The ten horns are kings who later hate the harlot and burn her (17:16), and the woman is 'the great city that rules over the kings of the earth' (17:18).","keyIssue":"Who is the harlot (Rome, Jerusalem, papal Rome, or a future system)? Do the seven kings let us date Revelation by identifying the king who 'is'? The date of Revelation is disputed: early (c. 65–68, before Jerusalem's fall) or late (c. 95, under Domitian, following Irenaeus).","timeText":false,"topics":["revelation-date","antichrist","great-tribulation","church-history"],"readings":[{"position":"partial-preterism","reading":"The seven kings are Roman emperors counted from Julius Caesar, as Josephus, Suetonius and others counted: Julius, Augustus, Tiberius, Gaius and Claudius have fallen; Nero (54–68) 'is'; Galba, who reigned only about seven months (68–69), is the one who 'remains a little while'. Revelation was therefore written under Nero, before AD 70. Many (Chilton, Gentry, Leithart) identify the harlot as apostate Jerusalem, allied with and riding on the Roman beast, which finally turned and destroyed her in AD 70 (17:16).","support":"Ancient historians (Josephus, Suetonius, Dio Cassius) count Julius as the first; the 'seven mountains' match Rome's famous seven hills, making the Roman reference explicit. Jerusalem is called the city where the Lord was crucified (11:8) and guilty of the blood of the prophets (Matthew 23:35–37; compare 18:24), and covenant infidelity is the prophets' meaning of 'harlot' (Ezekiel 16).","objection":"Late-date critics (e.g. Hitchcock) note that Augustus is often counted as the first emperor, that the city 'ruling over the kings of the earth' (17:18) is much more naturally Rome than Jerusalem, and that the external testimony of Irenaeus places Revelation under Domitian. Early-daters answer that Irenaeus's statement is ambiguous ('it was seen' may refer to John) and that internal evidence should prevail.","sources":["gentry-revelation-neros-time","gentry-before-jerusalem-fell","chilton-days-vengeance","robinson-redating-nt"]},{"position":"futurism","reading":"The seven heads are seven successive world empires that have oppressed God's people: commonly Egypt, Assyria, Babylon, Persia, Greece, Rome (the one that 'is' in John's day), and a future revived Roman confederacy; the eighth is the Antichrist's kingdom. The harlot is a future apostate religious-economic system centred in a literal city (some say rebuilt Babylon, others Rome). John wrote c. 95 under Domitian.","support":"'Mountains' are a common biblical symbol for kingdoms (Daniel 2:35; Jeremiah 51:25); the emperor-counting schemes are arbitrary because they must decide where to begin and whether to skip the three short reigns of 69; Irenaeus's testimony favours the late date.","objection":"Preterists respond that the angel explicitly explains the heads as the seven hills on which the woman sits, an unmistakable Roman landmark, and that 'kings' rather than 'kingdoms' is the angel's word; empire schemes also vary in their list.","sources":["hitchcock-rev-17-bsac","walvoord-revelation-jesus-christ","thomas-revelation-8-22"]},{"position":"idealism","reading":"The number seven is symbolic of completeness: the seven kings represent the whole series of anti-God world powers through history, with John's moment among them, near the end of the sequence. Babylon the harlot is the seductive world system of culture, commerce and false religion that tempts the church in every age, embodied in John's day by Rome.","support":"Revelation's numbers are consistently symbolic; the phrase 'he is an eighth and belongs to the seven' parodies Christ's resurrection; attempts to list emperors founder on which to count. Beale and Bauckham hold that the symbolic sense is primary, with Rome as the contemporary embodiment.","objection":"Preterists and futurists alike object that an angel's 'explanation' of the symbol (17:9–10) seems meant to give a concrete historical reference; idealists answer that the explanation itself is still in symbolic idiom ('this calls for a mind with wisdom').","sources":["beale-revelation-nigtc","bauckham-climax-prophecy","hendriksen-more-than-conquerors"]},{"position":"historicism","reading":"The harlot is papal Rome, the seven-hilled city that became the seat of a corrupt church allied with the kings of Europe; the seven heads are successive forms of Roman government (kings, consuls, decemvirs, dictators, military tribunes, emperors, and then a further form), with the beast's final head the papal power. The burning of the harlot by the ten kings is the eventual turning of European powers against the papacy.","support":"The Reformers and Protestant commentators (e.g. Barnes, Elliott) found in Rome's seven hills, its persecution of the saints and its alliance with kings a precise match for papal Rome; this reading influenced confessions and Protestant culture for centuries.","objection":"Critics say the scheme reads European church history into a book addressed to first-century Asian churches, and that historicist schemes have produced conflicting lists of heads and dates.","sources":["barnes-notes-revelation","elliott-horae"]},{"position":"amil","reading":"Many amillennialists combine idealism with a primary Roman reference: Babylon is Rome as the embodiment of the world-city opposed to God, and the seven kings symbolize the full sequence of persecuting powers in history; the final beast (the eighth) points to the culminating Antichrist power before Christ's return. They generally hold the late date (c. 95).","support":"Holds together the clear Roman markers and the book's symbolic numerology; supports the cyclical structure of Revelation.","objection":"Early-daters argue that a symbolic seven cannot explain the precise 'five, one, and one for a little while' sequence; futurists argue that symbolism does not preclude a future literal city.","sources":["dennis-johnson-triumph-lamb","riddlebarger-case-amil","beale-revelation-nigtc"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"Like partial preterism, the beast and harlot are Rome and apostate Jerusalem and the date is pre-70; full preterists add that the destruction of the harlot in AD 70 was the climax of all biblical eschatology, after which the Second Coming, resurrection and new creation of chs. 19–22 were also fulfilled.","support":"Uses the same seven-kings count and Jerusalem identification as partial preterists, with the time texts of 1:1 and 22:10 applied to the whole book.","objection":"Critics accept the historical reading of chapter 17 but reject extending it to chs. 19–22; the view's denial of a future Second Coming places it outside historic creedal orthodoxy.","sources":["russell-parousia","king-spirit-of-prophecy","mathison-when-shall"]}],"sources":["gentry-revelation-neros-time","hitchcock-rev-17-bsac","aune-revelation-wbc","irenaeus-against-heresies"]},{"id":"revelation-21-22-new-creation","title":"New Heaven, New Earth and the New Jerusalem","ref":"Revelation 21:1–8; 22:1–5","ranges":["Revelation 21:1-8","Revelation 22:1-5"],"category":"revelation","summary":"John sees a new heaven and a new earth, for the first heaven and earth have passed away and the sea is no more. The holy city, new Jerusalem, comes down from God like a bride, and a voice says that God's dwelling is with humanity; he will wipe away every tear, and death, mourning and pain will be no more (21:1–4). 'Behold, I am making all things new' (21:5). Outside are the cowardly, faithless and immoral, whose portion is the lake of fire (21:8). The river of the water of life flows from the throne, with the tree of life bearing fruit monthly and leaves for the healing of the nations; there is no more curse, and the servants see God's face and reign forever (22:1–5).","keyIssue":"Is this the eternal state after the final judgment, a picture of the new covenant community already present in history, or both? Does the new creation replace or renew the present world? Why are there 'nations' needing 'healing' (22:2)?","timeText":false,"topics":["kingdom","final-judgment","second-coming"],"readings":[{"position":"amil","reading":"The consummated new creation after Christ's return and the last judgment: the present world is renewed, not annihilated, and God dwells with his people forever. The new Jerusalem is the glorified church, the bride, in whom the promises of Eden, the temple and the land are fulfilled; aspects are already tasted now (Galatians 4:26; Hebrews 12:22), but the vision is of the final state.","support":"21:4 promises the end of death, which 1 Corinthians 15:26 places at the end; 21:8 locates the wicked in the lake of fire after the judgment of 20:11–15; 'making all things new' renews existing creation (Romans 8:21).","objection":"Postmillennial preterists argue that the vision's present-tense invitations (22:17) and 'healing of the nations' imply historical, not purely eternal, application; amillennialists answer that the healing is a picture of complete wholeness, not ongoing disease.","sources":["hoekema-bible-future","beale-revelation-nigtc","middleton-new-heaven-earth"]},{"position":"postmil","reading":"Many postmillennial preterists (e.g. Chilton, Gentry) read the new Jerusalem as the new covenant church, already established after the old Jerusalem's fall, whose influence brings 'healing of the nations' through history; this present reality will be consummated in the eternal state after Christ's return. Others read the vision chiefly of the eternal state while affirming its historical foretastes.","support":"Isaiah 65:17–25, the source of 21:1, describes a new creation that still includes death at a great age (65:20), suggesting a historical process; Hebrews 12:22 says believers 'have come' to the heavenly Jerusalem; the gates are open to the kings of the earth bringing their glory (21:24–26).","objection":"Amillennialists and premillennialists object that 21:4 (no more death) cannot describe the present age; postmillennialists respond that the vision blends the present reality with its consummate fulfilment, as is common in prophecy.","sources":["chilton-days-vengeance","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","mathison-postmillennialism"]},{"position":"disppremil","reading":"The eternal state after the millennium and the great white throne judgment, in which the new Jerusalem descends to a re-created earth. Some dispensationalists (e.g. Pentecost) also hold that the new Jerusalem exists during the millennium as the dwelling of the resurrected saints, above or in relation to the earthly kingdom, and 21:9–22:5 may describe it in that role.","support":"The sequence of Revelation 19–21 (return, millennium, judgment, new creation) is read chronologically; the distinction between the earthly millennial Jerusalem and the heavenly new Jerusalem preserves both Israel's land promises and the church's heavenly hope.","objection":"Critics argue that the 'healing of the nations' and a city descending 'from heaven' are symbolic portraits of one final reality, and that a millennial new Jerusalem confuses the book's sequence.","sources":["pentecost-things-to-come","walvoord-revelation-jesus-christ"]},{"position":"histpremil","reading":"The eternal state that follows Christ's millennial reign and the last judgment: a renewed earth, not a disembodied heaven, where God dwells with his people. The millennium is the first stage of Christ's victory on earth, and the new creation is its final stage.","support":"Ladd stressed the continuity between the present earth and the new earth, and the Old Testament hope of an earthly kingdom. The millennium explains why Revelation 20 places an earthly reign of the saints (20:4; compare 5:10) before the new creation of 21:1.","objection":"Amillennialists argue that once the new creation is admitted, a separate millennium becomes redundant.","sources":["ladd-commentary-revelation","ladd-blessed-hope"]},{"position":"full-preterism","reading":"The new heaven and new earth are the new covenant world established when the old covenant order (the 'first heaven and earth' and the 'sea' of the nations) passed away in AD 70; the new Jerusalem is the church, now dwelling with God, and its open gates (22:17) invite the nations today. There is no future cosmic renewal.","support":"The book's time markers (22:6, 10, 12) are placed right after the new creation vision; Isaiah 65:20 shows a 'new heavens and earth' that still includes death, and 22:15 shows sinners still outside the city.","objection":"Critics reply that 21:4 promises the end of death, mourning and pain, which have not ceased; that 21:8 places the wicked in the lake of fire; and that the denial of a future bodily resurrection and new creation places the view outside historic creedal orthodoxy.","sources":["king-spirit-of-prophecy","preston-eschatology-org","mathison-when-shall"]},{"position":"idealism","reading":"A symbolic portrait of the perfected people of God and their final fellowship with him, drawn from Eden, Ezekiel's temple and Isaiah's new creation. The cube-shaped city (21:16) recalls the Holy of Holies, so the whole community is God's sanctuary.","support":"Beale shows how the city is a temple-garden fulfilling Ezekiel 40–48 and Genesis 2; the descriptive details (gold like glass, pearl gates) are visionary.","objection":"Some ask whether the symbolic reading loses the physicality of the new creation; idealists answer that the symbols point to a real, embodied future.","sources":["beale-revelation-nigtc","hendriksen-more-than-conquerors","beale-temple-church-mission"]}],"sources":["beale-revelation-nigtc","middleton-new-heaven-earth","pentecost-things-to-come","chilton-days-vengeance"]}]`),i=t,s=new Map(i.map(e=>[e.id,e]));export{i as a,s as p};
